IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `family-domain`

§ `Probate and Administration Act 1934 — the grant machinery`

-- ===========================================================================
-- PROBATE AND ADMINISTRATION ACT 1934 (Singapore), 2020 Revised Edition,
-- Informal Consolidation, version in force from 15/10/2024.
--
-- WHAT THIS MODULE ENCODES. The GRANT MACHINERY: who may be granted probate
-- or letters of administration, in what number, subject to what security, and
-- what the Public Trustee may do where the estate is small. Concretely —
--
-- THIS INDEX ENUMERATES SUBSECTIONS AND NOT SECTIONS, and it agrees with the
-- closing "What is NOT encoded, and why" section. A subsection missing from a
-- range below is a subsection that section names as not encoded. The two lists
-- were checked against each other by walking the index range by range and
-- grepping the closing section for each gap: an earlier draft made this claim
-- while s 56(1), s 62(4) and s 64(2)-(3) were named only at their own sites
-- and not in the closing list, so the claim was true of every range but three.
-- Group (4) of that section is where those three now sit.
--
--   s 2               the definitions the grant machinery reads
--   s 5(1)            the effect of renunciation
--   s 6(1),(2),(4)-(6) the number of personal representatives
--                     (s 6(3), the evidential power, is carried inert)
--   s 8(1)-(3)        grant of probate
--   ss 9-11           probate where the will is lost, destroyed or proved abroad
--   s 12, s 16        a codicil propounded after a grant
--   s 13(1)-(2)       letters of administration with the will annexed, on
--                     failure of executors, and s 13(2)'s RANKED class — the
--                     one ladder in the grant machinery, "in the following
--                     order". (Not the only ranked list in the Act: the Second
--                     Schedule, given effect by s 57(4), is headed "ORDER OF
--                     APPLICATION OF ASSETS WHERE THE ESTATE IS SOLVENT" and
--                     runs 1 to 7. It is out of scope, and named as such in
--                     the closing section.)
--   ss 14-15          a grant to the attorney of an absent executor or claimant
--   s 17              a grant limited until the will is produced
--   s 18(1),(2),(4),(5) grants on intestacy, and the classes s 18(4) opens —
--                     which are a LIST and not a ladder; see the note there
--   s 19              a grant to the attorney of an absent person entitled
--   s 20              a grant pending a probate action
--   s 21(1)-(3)       infants
--   s 22(1)-(2)       persons who lack mental capacity
--   ss 23-24          grants limited to trust property, and to collection
--   s 25(1)-(2)       death of one of several personal representatives
--   s 26(2)           a further grant of the excepted part
--   s 27              administration after a limited grant has expired
--   s 29(2),(4),(5),(7),(8) security: when a bond is required, and how many
--                     sureties (s 29(1), (3) and (6) are discretions)
--   s 30              the bond of a creditor-administrator
--   s 35(1)           grants in excess of a District Court's jurisdiction
--   s 55(1)-(2)       grant to the Public Trustee in cases of delay
--   s 56(2)           the $500 small-payee route
--   s 62(1)-(3)       the Public Trustee's small-estate power, its threshold
--                     and the deeming that follows his declaration
--   s 63(1)-(2)       payment for a minor's maintenance, and its thresholds
--   s 64(1)           the $10,000 exemption from notice of distribution
--
-- It is NOT a formalisation of the whole Act. The section headed "What is NOT
-- encoded, and why" at the foot of this file names every provision left out
-- and gives the reason for each. A reader who cannot tell a provision that is
-- not in the Act from one that is merely not encoded has been misled, so that
-- section is part of the deliverable and not an appendix to it.
--
-- SOURCE. Every quoted string in this file is copied mechanically out of
-- source/PAA1934.txt in this directory, a `pdftotext -layout` extraction of
-- the official SSO PDF, with the running headers, page numbers, amendment
-- markers and "Informal Consolidation" watermark lines discarded and the
-- line-wrapping normalised to single spaces. The source's curly quotation
-- marks and apostrophes are preserved as they stand there. No quotation was
-- reconstructed from memory.
--
-- ONTOLOGY. The nouns come from `family-domain.l4`, which four Act modules
-- share. Nothing it declares is redeclared here. Where this Act reads a fact
-- that module does not carry, the fact is declared below in
-- `The further facts on which the grant machinery turns` and reported as a
-- domain gap rather than smuggled into the shared file.
--
-- HOUSE STYLE. Inert: nothing is @export'ed. `GIVEN`, never `ASSUME`.
-- `BRANCH ... IF ... OTHERWISE` for multi-way choices. One decision per
-- provision, named after the provision's own words, so that a reader holding
-- the Act can find the rule from the section heading. Verbatim statutory
-- prose rides as inert strings, `...` is AND-sugar and `..` is OR-sugar.
-- Citations ride on @ref.
--
-- TIME. Many of this Act's questions are asked AS AT A MOMENT — s 21(1) bars
-- a grant "while he is an infant", s 55(1)(a) counts six months "from the
-- death", s 6(4) runs "during the minority of a beneficiary". A decision that
-- asks such a question takes an explicit `the date` parameter rather than
-- reading a clock, so that the same file evaluates the same way tomorrow.
--
-- AMBIGUITY REGISTER. Twelve interpretive forks are marked `AMBIGUITY A1` to
-- `A12` at the site where each bites, with both readings, the reading taken
-- and why. They are tabulated again in the closing section; the table and
-- these comments must agree. Two questions that LOOK like forks and are not —
-- s 6(4)'s narrower minority, and which grants s 29(8) reaches — are noted
-- with them so that a reader hunting the fork finds the answer.
--
-- COVERAGE IS MEASURED BY MUTATION, NOT BY COUNTING ASSERTIONS. Every conjunct
-- and disjunct in every decision below was deleted, one at a time, and the
-- suite re-run; a conjunct whose deletion leaves the suite green has no test,
-- however many assertions surround it. Three such conjuncts remain and each is
-- named, with the reason it cannot be isolated, at the head of the worked
-- examples. (Four, after the s 21(2) repair — see there.) Negative assertions that are over-determined — where more than one
-- limb fails on the fixture — are labelled as such beside the minimal pair
-- that does test the limb.
-- ===========================================================================


§§ `Section 2 — Interpretation`

-- MOST OF s 2 IS ALREADY IN THE SHARED ONTOLOGY and is not restated here.
-- "court" is `Court`; the three grant definitions are `Kind of grant`; the
-- Public Trustee and a licensed trust company are members of
-- `Person or corporation`; "will" includes any codicil, which is why `Will`
-- carries `the codicils` as a `LIST OF Will`.
--
-- What follows is the residue: the six definitions that the grant machinery
-- reads and that the ontology does not name in those words, carried inert so
-- the definitional chain is visible and auditable at the place the Act puts it.
-- "will" appears here as well as in the ontology because s 2's inclusive words
-- are what make `Will`'s `the codicils` field answer to this Act; "prescribed
-- form" appears because ss 28(1), 29(2) and 56(2) all read it and nothing in
-- this module computes it — the form is rules of court, and the closing
-- section names that as the reason Part 4 stops at the duty.

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 2
`s 2 — the definitions the grant machinery reads` MEANS
        "“prescribed form” means the form prescribed by rules made under any law for the time being in force relating to the courts;"
    ... "“probate action” means a cause or matter in which a probate application is contested by any person, and includes any application to alter or revoke the grant of any probate or letters of administration;"
    ... "“probate application” means an application for a grant of probate or letters of administration, and “probate applicant” shall be construed accordingly;"
    ... "“Public Trustee” means the Public Trustee appointed under the Public Trustee Act 1915 and includes a Deputy Public Trustee and an Assistant Public Trustee appointed under that Act;"
    ... "“registrar” means the registrar of the Family Justice Courts;"
    ... "“will” includes any codicil or other testamentary document."


§§ `The further facts on which the grant machinery turns`

-- FIVE FACTS THIS ACT READS THAT THE SHARED ONTOLOGY DOES NOT CARRY. They are
-- declared here, in this Act's own module, rather than added to
-- `family-domain.l4`, because three other Act modules are relying on that file
-- being stable. Each is reported as a domain gap.
--
-- All five are FACTS, not discretions: each is something a witness could
-- depose to once the moment has passed — what the Family Court did, what the
-- Public Trustee signed, what the estate is worth net of debts. The
-- DISCRETIONS they sit beside (s 29(3)'s "for any sufficient reason", s 62(1)'s
-- "if he thinks fit", s 55's "such other person as the court thinks fit") are
-- not recorded anywhere, and are named in the closing section instead.
@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 29(5), s 29(8)(b), s 39, s 55(1)(d), s 62(1), s 62(3)
DECLARE `The further facts on which the grant machinery turns` HAS
    @ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 29(5) — "When the administrator is entitled to the whole of the estate after payment of the debts, sureties in the bond may ordinarily be dispensed with"; Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 7
    -- DOMAIN GAP, AND IT IS A JOIN THAT DOES NOT CONNECT. `Entitlement` is
    -- declared in `family-domain.l4` and no field anywhere has its type, so no
    -- route runs from `Deceased person` to who is entitled to what. Until one
    -- does, s 29(5)'s antecedent arrives here as a proved fact rather than as a
    -- computation over the Intestate Succession Act's answer.
    --
    -- THE OTHER SIDE DOES COMPUTE IT. `intestate-succession-act.l4` produces
    -- `LIST OF Entitlement` from its s 7 rules — the same fact under another
    -- name, since "the administrator is entitled to the whole of the estate"
    -- is a statement about the distribution that Act governs. Because the two
    -- meet nowhere, a case file may swear this boolean TRUE while that Act's
    -- rules give the administrator half, and nothing in this cleanroom would
    -- object. The consequence is not small: on that contradiction the sureties
    -- on an administration bond are dispensed with in a case where s 29(5)
    -- does not permit it.
    --
    -- WHAT WOULD CLOSE IT, and it is not this module's to do: `Deceased person`
    -- would need a field of type `LIST OF Entitlement`, and then s 29(5)'s
    -- antecedent would be a computation over it — the whole of the estate,
    -- after the debts, to one person who is the administrator. Reported as a
    -- domain gap against the shared ontology rather than patched here, because
    -- three other Act modules are relying on that file being stable, and
    -- because a fact with two homes that can disagree is worse than a fact
    -- with one home that is known to be an input.
    `the administrator is entitled to the whole of the estate after payment of the debts` IS A BOOLEAN

    @ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 29(8)(b) — "the Family Court thinks fit to require such security"
    -- The EXERCISE of the s 29(8)(b) discretion, not the discretion itself:
    -- once the Family Court has said so, whether it said so is a fact.
    `the Family Court thinks fit to require such security` IS A BOOLEAN

    @ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 39 — "that there is danger that the property may be wasted"
    -- s 55(1)(d) asks whether "a receiver may be appointed under section 39",
    -- and s 39's own gate is this. Part 9 is otherwise out of scope.
    `there is danger that the property may be wasted` IS A BOOLEAN

    @ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 55(1)(d) — "but it appears that such appointment would not be a sufficient protection for the estate"
    `it appears that such appointment would not be a sufficient protection for the estate` IS A BOOLEAN

    @ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 62(1) — "by writing signed by him declare that he undertakes to administer such property"; s 62(3)
    -- s 62(3)'s antecedent ("Upon a declaration under subsection (1)") is the
    -- fact that the writing was signed, which nothing in the ontology records.
    `the Public Trustee has by writing signed by him declared that he undertakes to administer such property` IS A BOOLEAN


§§ `Reading the shared ontology — the helpers the sections below share`

-- No section of the Act corresponds to anything in this subsection. These are
-- the readings of `family-domain.l4` that more than one provision needs, named
-- once so that the provisions themselves stay short and so that a ruling about
-- an absent fact is made in ONE place and can be found.

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 6(2), s 13(1)(c), s 25(1)
GIVEN pc IS A `Person or corporation`
GIVETH A MAYBE Person
`the individual in` pc MEANS
    CONSIDER pc
    WHEN `an individual` p THEN JUST p
    OTHERWISE NOTHING

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 6(2) — "or to not less than 2 individuals"
GIVEN pc IS A `Person or corporation`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`an individual, within the meaning of section 6(2)` pc MEANS
    isJust (`the individual in` pc)

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 6(6) — "For the purposes of this section, “trust corporation” means the Public Trustee or a corporation licensed as a trust company under the Trust Companies Act 2005."
-- s 6(6) is a definition, so it is a decision of its own and the two limbs are
-- the two non-individual, non-Government members of `Person or corporation`.
-- `the Government` is NOT a trust corporation: it is in that type because the
-- Intestate Succession Act's rule 9 needs it, and s 6(6) does not name it.
GIVEN pc IS A `Person or corporation`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 6(6) — a trust corporation` pc MEANS
    CONSIDER pc
    WHEN `the Public Trustee` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `a corporation licensed as a trust company under the Trust Companies Act 2005` n THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 13(1)(d), s 25(1), s 55(1)(f)
GIVEN pc IS A `Person or corporation`, `the reference` IS A STRING
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
pc `is the individual whose reference is` `the reference` MEANS
    CONSIDER pc
    WHEN `an individual` p THEN p's `the reference for this person` EQUALS `the reference`
    OTHERWISE FALSE

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 13(1)(c)-(d), s 25(1), s 55(1)(f)
-- HOW AN UNDATED DEATH IS READ, decided once, here. `Vital status` has three
-- members and the third — `dead, the date of death not known` — exists exactly
-- so that a module has to rule on it rather than default silently. The ruling:
-- a person known to be dead is dead for every provision that asks only THAT
-- (s 13(1)(d) "all the executors die", s 25(1) "one of them dies"), and is NOT
-- shown to survive anyone for a provision that asks WHEN (s 13(1)(c)).
GIVEN p IS A Person
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`has died` p MEANS
    CONSIDER p's `vital status`
    WHEN `living` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

GIVEN p IS A Person
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`the date of death of` p MEANS
    CONSIDER p's `vital status`
    WHEN `died on` d THEN JUST d
    OTHERWISE NOTHING

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 13(1)(c) — "no executor survives the testator"; s 25(1) — "the representation of the estate shall accrue to the surviving executor or executors"
-- AMBIGUITY A1 — what "survives" requires when a date of death is missing.
--   Reading (i): survivorship is a fact to be PROVED, so a person whose death
--     is known but undated is not shown to have survived anybody, and a
--     testator whose death is undated cannot be outlived.
--   Reading (ii): survivorship is presumed from the order in which the facts
--     were recorded, or from the person being alive at the date of the
--     application.
-- TAKEN: reading (i). s 13(1)(c) is one limb of a gate that OPENS a grant of
-- letters of administration with the will annexed; a limb not proved is a limb
-- not made out, and reading (ii) would let an unproved fact open the gate.
GIVEN a IS A Person, b IS A Person
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
a `survives` b MEANS
    CONSIDER a's `vital status`
    WHEN `living` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `dead, the date of death not known` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `died on` da THEN
        CONSIDER b's `vital status`
        WHEN `died on` db THEN da `is after` db
        OTHERWISE FALSE

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 21(1) — "while he is an infant"; s 21(2)-(3), s 6(2), s 29(8)(a), s 63(1)
-- AMBIGUITY A2 — a person whose date of majority is not in evidence.
--   Reading (i): infancy is not established, so the s 21(1) bar does not bite
--     and no minority is found for s 6(2).
--   Reading (ii): the protective provisions (s 6(2), s 29(8)(a), s 63) should
--     assume infancy until majority is proved, since they exist to protect the
--     infant and the cost of a wrong answer is asymmetric.
-- TAKEN: reading (i), uniformly, and s 6(3) is the reason it can be taken
-- without loss: "The court in granting letters of administration may act on
-- such prima facie evidence, furnished by the applicant or any other person,
-- as to whether or not there is a minority or life interest, as may be
-- prescribed by rules made under any written law for the time being in force
-- relating to the courts." The Act itself routes the EVIDENTIAL question to
-- the court and to rules of court, so a module that answered it from silence
-- would be substituting for a forum the Act names. Reading (ii) is the safer
-- default for a drafter and is recorded so that a consumer of this module can
-- adopt it by supplying the date.
--
-- WHAT THE FOLD COSTS, STATED PLAINLY. Folding NOTHING to FALSE means an
-- unproved age passes the s 21(1) bar. s 21(1) is a prohibition and this
-- module encodes it as one, but the encoding does NOT put the burden of
-- proving full age on the person propounding the grant: on these three states
-- of the evidence — proved infant, proved adult, not in evidence — the bar
-- bites on the first only. A drafter who wants the burden the other way round
-- has to change this decision and not merely the phrasing of s 21(1) below.
-- An earlier draft of the s 21(1) note asserted that burden as though the
-- encoding carried it; the note is corrected at its own site.
--
-- ***** THE NEIGHBOURING ACTS ANSWER THIS SAME QUESTION THREE WAYS *****
-- The evidential question — what to do when a person's age is not in evidence
-- — arises in three of the four modules, and each resolves it differently.
-- Recorded here because a reader who finds only one of the three arguments
-- will think it uncontested, and it is not.
--   THE WILLS ACT SAYS THE OPPOSITE. `the age of the testator when the will
--   was made` in `wills-act.l4` returns a THREE-member enum whose third member
--   is `not in evidence, the date of birth of the testator or the date of
--   execution of the will not being known`, and its AMBIGUITY A1 takes the
--   reading that "validity must be affirmatively established", so an unproved
--   age FAILS the limb there. Both readings are defensible; the difference is
--   not an oversight on either side and it turns on a real difference between
--   the two Acts. Wills Act s 4 is a validity condition with a number in it
--   ("No will made by any person under 21 years of age shall be valid") and
--   that Act names no forum to resolve the evidence; this Act's s 6(3) names
--   one, and names rules of court to govern it. Where the Act supplies a
--   forum, a module that answered from silence would be substituting for it;
--   where it supplies none, the burden has to fall somewhere and the
--   propounder is where the Wills Act puts it.
--   THE GUARDIANSHIP ACT REFUSES TO CHOOSE. `the person is an infant on the
--   date in question` in `guardianship-of-infants-act.l4` returns
--   `MAYBE BOOLEAN` and yields NOTHING on the same input. That is the honest
--   shape and this module cannot use it: `is an infant on` feeds four
--   provisions (s 6(2), s 21, s 29(8)(a), s 63(1)) whose outputs are gates,
--   and a gate has to open or close.
--   MEASURED, THE TWO THAT DEFER TO THE SHARED FIELD AGREE EXACTLY. Where the
--   majority date IS in evidence, this module and the Guardianship Act module
--   read the same `Person` field and compare it the same strict way, boundary
--   included: on a majority date of 1 June 2032, both answer infant on 31 May
--   and not on 1 June. The pair below asserts this module's half of that.
--
-- A DATE, not a boolean: s 21(1) bars a grant "while he is an infant" and
-- s 21(2) limits one "until the infant obtains a grant to himself". Infancy
-- ends, and a timeless flag cannot say when.
GIVEN p IS A Person, `the date` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
p `is an infant on` `the date` MEANS
    CONSIDER p's `the date on which the person attains the age of majority`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN FALSE
    WHEN JUST m  THEN `the date` `is before` m

@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 3 — "“child” means a legitimate child and includes any child adopted by virtue of an order of court under any written law for the time being in force in Singapore, Malaysia or Brunei Darussalam"; Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 18(4)(a)
-- AMBIGUITY A12 — whether the Intestate Succession Act 1967's DEFINITION of
-- "child" travels into this Act's "next of kin", or only its CLASSES do.
-- AMBIGUITY A3 below settles that the classes are that Act's and settles that
-- they are taken flat; it never asked whether the persons who fill them are
-- identified that Act's way. They are two questions and this is the second.
--   Reading (i): the definition travels with the classes. A3 borrows "the
--     classes to whom the Intestate Succession Act 1967 distributes on an
--     intestacy", and a person to whom that Act distributes NOTHING does not
--     fill one of its classes. Its s 3 confines "child" to "a legitimate child"
--     and an adopted child, and its s 3 "“issue” includes children and the
--     descendants of deceased children" is built on that word, so an
--     illegitimate unadopted child is outside rule 3 and takes nothing.
--   Reading (ii): the definition does not travel. Intestate Succession Act
--     s 3 opens "In this Act —", which confines it by its own terms; "next of
--     kin" is a phrase about KINSHIP, and kinship in fact does not depend on
--     legitimacy.
-- TAKEN: reading (i), and it is applied at every generation, so a branch of
-- the descent that fails s 3 drops entire rather than being stepped over.
-- THREE REASONS.
--   First, A3's own reasoning commits to it. A3 does not read "next of kin" as
--   blood relationship at large; it reads it as the Intestate Succession Act's
--   distributive classes, and gives textual reasons from s 18(4)(b) and
--   s 18(4)(d)(i) for taking those classes flat. A module cannot borrow a
--   class by the name of the Act that defines it and then populate it against
--   that Act.
--   Second, the historic sense of the term of art is entitlement and not
--   blood, and s 18(4)(a) shows this Act using it that way: it says "the
--   husband or widow or next of kin", naming the spouse SEPARATELY, which is
--   how "next of kin" reads under a statute of distributions — the surviving
--   spouse takes but is not of the kin. The separate naming is evidence for
--   reading (i) and not against it.
--   Third, every provision that reads this class reads it as a class of
--   persons with a stake: s 18(4)(a) opens a grant to them, s 18(4)(d)(i)
--   asks what they "so desire", s 18(4)(d)(ii) asks whether one "makes out a
--   claim". A person the Intestate Succession Act pays nothing has no stake to
--   make out.
--
-- THE WILLS ACT MODULE TAKES THE OPPOSITE READING ON ITS OWN s 26 AND IS RIGHT
-- TO. `the donee, so far as he is a child or other issue of the testator` in
-- wills-act.l4 says in terms that s 26's "a child or other issue of the
-- testator" is descent and NOT the Intestate Succession Act's defined term,
-- "because ISA s 3 opens \"In this Act\" and defines \"child\" for the
-- Intestate Succession Act alone". The two modules are not in conflict:
-- Wills Act s 26 defines its own subject ("a child or other issue") and
-- borrows nothing, so the confinement in "In this Act" is the end of the
-- matter there. This Act names no class at all and A3 fills the gap by
-- pointing at the Intestate Succession Act; having pointed, it takes what is
-- there. The divergence is the difference between a phrase that stands on its
-- own words and a phrase that has been construed by reference to another Act.
--
-- WHY THE TWO LIMBS ARE RESTATED HERE AND THE OTHER MODULE IS NOT IMPORTED.
-- `intestate-succession-act.l4` states this same definition as
-- `section 3 — a “child” within the meaning of this Act`. This module does not
-- import it: an Act module in this cleanroom imports the shared ontology and
-- nothing else, and a grant machinery that could not be typechecked without
-- the distribution rules would be the wrong dependency to introduce for a
-- two-field test. The two limbs are `Child`'s own fields, which the ontology
-- carries for exactly this purpose. If the two ever disagree the shared
-- ontology is the place to reconcile them; that is reported as a domain gap.
GIVEN c IS A Child
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a “child” within the meaning of section 3 of the Intestate Succession Act 1967` c MEANS
        c's `a legitimate child of the person whose child he is`
    OR  c's `adopted by that person by virtue of an order of court under any written law for the time being in force in Singapore, Malaysia or Brunei Darussalam`

@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 7 rule 3 and Proviso No. (2); Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 18(4)(a)
GIVEN p IS A Person
GIVETH A LIST OF Person
`the descendants of` p MEANS
    concat (map `the child and his descendants` (p's `children`))

@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 7 rule 3 Proviso No. (1) — "The persons who legally represent the children of an intestate are their descendants and not their next-of-kin."; s 3
-- THE WHOLE BRANCH DROPS, and Proviso No. (1) is why. Representation runs
-- through THE CHILDREN of the intestate — "such persons as legally represent
-- those children" — so a person who is not a "child" within Intestate
-- Succession Act s 3 forms no stock for anyone to stand in the place of.
-- Stepping over him and keeping his issue would put a grandchild in a
-- position the Act gives him only per stirpes through a parent who is not
-- there. See AMBIGUITY A12 above for the ruling this applies.
GIVEN c IS A Child
GIVETH A LIST OF Person
`the child and his descendants` c MEANS
    IF   `a “child” within the meaning of section 3 of the Intestate Succession Act 1967` c
    THEN (c's `the child`) FOLLOWED BY (`the descendants of` (c's `the child`))
    ELSE EMPTY

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 18(4)(a) — "the husband or widow or next of kin or any of them"; s 18(4)(d)(i); s 13(2)(c)
-- AMBIGUITY A3 — who "the next of kin" are. The Probate and Administration
-- Act uses the phrase three times (s 18(4)(a), s 18(4)(d)(i), s 18(4)(d)(ii))
-- and never defines it.
--   Reading (i): the classes to whom the Intestate Succession Act 1967
--     distributes on an intestacy — issue, parents, brothers and sisters and
--     their issue, grandparents, uncles and aunts — taken as a flat class.
--   Reading (ii): the NEAREST such class only, the ranking of the Intestate
--     Succession Act being carried across into the grant.
-- TAKEN: reading (i). s 18(4)(b) puts it "in the discretion of the court to
-- grant them to any one or more of such persons", which presupposes that the
-- class is wider than one rung; and s 18(4)(d)(i) COUNTS the next of kin ("or
-- the greater number of such next of kin"), which a single-rung reading would
-- make turn on the accident of which rung is occupied. The ranking that
-- reading (ii) wants is the Intestate Succession Act's, and this Act does not
-- import it.
--
-- WHAT A3 DOES NOT DECIDE, AND A12 DOES. A3 settles WHICH classes and settles
-- that they are flat. It does not settle whether the persons who FILL those
-- classes are identified the Intestate Succession Act's way — whether that
-- Act's s 3 definition of "child" travels with the classes A3 borrows. It
-- does: see AMBIGUITY A12 at
-- `a “child” within the meaning of section 3 of the Intestate Succession Act 1967`
-- above, where the fork is stated and taken. An earlier draft of this module
-- asserted A3 in these words and then walked the raw `children` edge with no
-- filter at all, so a person the Intestate Succession Act pays nothing was
-- counted among the classes to which it distributes — this module opened a
-- grant to him under s 18(4)(a) and counted him a s 6(4) beneficiary while
-- that Act gave him no share. The contradiction was between this comment and
-- the code beneath it, not between the two Acts.
--
-- THE SPOUSE IS NOT HERE. s 18(4)(a) names "the husband or widow" separately
-- from "next of kin", so folding the spouse into this list would make the
-- Act's own disjunction redundant. That separate naming is also the second of
-- A12's reasons: it is how the phrase reads as a term of art of distribution,
-- under which the surviving spouse takes but is not of the kin.
--
-- A PREDECEASED RELATIVE IS NOT NEXT OF KIN, and the survivorship filter below
-- is the same one `the husband or widow of the deceased` applies one function
-- later. Three of the six provisions that read this list make the point
-- unarguable: s 18(4)(a) opens a grant "to the husband or widow or next of kin
-- or any of them", and a grant cannot be made to a dead person; s 18(4)(d)(i)
-- asks whether "the next of kin ... or the greater number of such next of kin,
-- so desire", and a dead relative can neither desire nor be counted in the
-- denominator against those who do; s 19 asks whether a person entitled "is
-- absent from Singapore". An earlier draft filtered the spouse limb and not
-- this one, so a child who died before his father was still counted as next of
-- kin and his silence defeated the unanimous wish of the living. AMBIGUITY A1
-- governs the undated death: a person known to be dead but not shown to have
-- died after the deceased is not shown to have survived him, and drops out.
GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A LIST OF Person
`the next of kin of the deceased` d MEANS
    filter (GIVEN p YIELD (p `survives` (d's `the deceased`)))
           (`the classes from which the next of kin are drawn` d)

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 18(4)(a)
-- The classes themselves, before the survivorship filter. Kept as a decision
-- of its own so that the classes and the filter can be read apart.
GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A LIST OF Person
`the classes from which the next of kin are drawn` d MEANS
    concat
      (LIST `the descendants of` (d's `the deceased`)
          , maybeToList (d's `the family`'s `the father of the deceased`)
          , maybeToList (d's `the family`'s `the mother of the deceased`)
          , map (GIVEN r YIELD r's `the relative`) (d's `the family`'s `the brothers and sisters of the deceased`)
          , concat (map (GIVEN r YIELD `the descendants of` (r's `the relative`))
                        (d's `the family`'s `the brothers and sisters of the deceased`))
          , d's `the family`'s `the grandparents of the deceased`
          , map (GIVEN r YIELD r's `the relative`) (d's `the family`'s `the uncles and aunts of the deceased`)
      )

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 18(4)(a) — "the husband or widow"; s 56(2) — "widower, widow"
-- A marriage counts only if it was subsisting at the death (not dissolved or
-- annulled before it) AND the spouse outlived the deceased: "husband or widow"
-- is a survivor, and `Marriage`'s subsistence field deliberately carries only
-- the first of those two facts.
GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`, m IS A Marriage
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a marriage leaving a husband or widow` d m MEANS
        m's `subsisting at the death of the deceased, in the sense of not dissolved or annulled before the death`
    AND ((m's `the spouse`) `survives` (d's `the deceased`))

GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A LIST OF Person
`the husband or widow of the deceased` d MEANS
    map (GIVEN m YIELD m's `the spouse`)
        (filter (GIVEN m YIELD `a marriage leaving a husband or widow` d m)
                (d's `the family`'s `the marriages of the deceased`))

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 62(1), s 64(1)
-- "property in Singapore" (s 62(1)) and the base of s 64(1)'s total, before
-- either section's own exclusions are taken off.
GIVEN e IS AN Estate
GIVETH A Money
`the value of the property in Singapore` e MEANS
        e's `the value of the movable property situated in Singapore`
    PLUS e's `the value of the immovable property situated in Singapore`

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 6(4), s 13(1)(d), s 23, s 25(1)
GIVEN gr IS A Grantee
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a personal representative` gr MEANS
        gr's `the capacity in which the grant is made` EQUALS `as executor`
    OR  gr's `the capacity in which the grant is made` EQUALS `as administrator`

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 6(4) — "If there is only one personal representative"; s 25(1)
-- Over the estate rather than over one grant: s 6(4) counts the representatives
-- the estate has, and s 26(2) and s 27 both contemplate more than one grant.
-- A revoked grant (s 32) leaves no representative standing, so it is filtered
-- out first.
GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A LIST OF Grantee
`the personal representatives of the estate` d MEANS
    filter (GIVEN gr YIELD `a personal representative` gr)
           (concat (map (GIVEN g YIELD g's `the grantees`)
                        (filter (GIVEN g YIELD NOT (g's `revoked`))
                                (d's `the grants made in respect of the estate`))))

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 6(4) — "If there is only one personal representative"; s 25(1)
-- TWO READINGS, AND THE ACT NEEDS BOTH. The list above counts every grantee
-- under an unrevoked grant, DEAD OR ALIVE, and s 55(1)(f) needs it that way:
-- its subject is "the death of a last surviving executor or administrator", so
-- a list that dropped the dead would leave it nothing to speak of.
--
-- s 6(4) NEEDS THE OTHER READING, and the Act says why itself. s 25(1) —
-- "Where probate or letters of administration have been granted to more than
-- one executor or administrator, and one of them dies, the representation of
-- the estate shall accrue to the surviving executor or executors" — means that
-- after such a death the estate has ONE personal representative, not two. An
-- earlier draft counted the dead one, so an estate with an infant beneficiary
-- and a single surviving administrator reported the very s 6(4) window shut
-- that exists to open in exactly that case: the module encoded the accrual two
-- hundred lines below and then contradicted it here.
--
-- The corporate default of `this grantee has died` (stated at s 25(1)) governs
-- here too: a trust corporation is never dropped from this list.
GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A LIST OF Grantee
`the personal representatives of the estate still living` d MEANS
    filter (GIVEN gr YIELD NOT (`this grantee has died` gr))
           (`the personal representatives of the estate` d)


§§ `Part 2 — Renunciation`

-- ss 3 and 4 (express and constructive renunciation, the citation and the
-- deemed renunciation for failing to file a notice of intention to contest)
-- are NOT encoded: they are procedural machinery that PRODUCES a fact, and the
-- fact itself is already in the shared ontology as `Executor appointed by a
-- will`'s `has renounced the right to act as such`. What IS encoded is s 5(1),
-- because it is that fact's operative consequence.

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 5(1) — "The renunciation, whether made expressly in the manner provided by section 3, or constructively in the manner provided by section 4, shall preclude the person so renouncing from applying thereafter for probate or letters of administration."
-- s 5(2) is a discretion — "the court may at any time allow the person so
-- renouncing to withdraw his renunciation ... if it is shown that the
-- withdrawal is for the benefit of the estate" — and is NOT encoded. So this
-- decision states the s 5(1) preclusion on its own terms; a withdrawal allowed
-- under s 5(2) is expressed in the ontology by the renunciation fact ceasing
-- to be true, not by a second rule here.
--
-- s 5(1) SPEAKS OF THE RENOUNCER WITHOUT QUALIFICATION — "the person so
-- renouncing" and no more — and the
-- class of renouncers is s 3(1)'s: "Any person who is or may become entitled to
-- any probate or letters of administration may expressly renounce his right to
-- such grant." On an intestacy that is a widow or a next of kin, and neither is
-- an executor appointed by a will. So the subsection is stated once, over the
-- proved fact, at the generality s 3(1) gives it.
GIVEN `the person has renounced, whether expressly under section 3 or constructively under section 4` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 5(1) — the person so renouncing is precluded from applying thereafter for probate or letters of administration` MEANS
    `the person has renounced, whether expressly under section 3 or constructively under section 4`

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 5(1)
-- DOMAIN GAP — RENUNCIATION HAS ONLY ONE HOME IN THE SHARED ONTOLOGY, and it is
-- on an executor: `Executor appointed by a will`'s `has renounced the right to
-- act as such`. `Person` carries no such field, so for anyone who is not an
-- appointed executor — the widow or next of kin whom s 3(1) plainly reaches —
-- the fact has to be supplied to the decision above as a GIVEN and cannot be
-- read off a case file. Reported rather than papered over; the fix is for
-- `Person` to carry the renunciation, since s 3(1) attaches it to a person and
-- not to an office.
GIVEN x IS AN `Executor appointed by a will`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 5(1) — precluded from applying thereafter for probate or letters of administration` x MEANS
    `s 5(1) — the person so renouncing is precluded from applying thereafter for probate or letters of administration`
        (x's `has renounced the right to act as such`)


§ `Part 3 — Grant of probate or letters of administration`

§§ `Section 6 — Provisions as to number of personal representatives`

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 6(1) — "Probate or letters of administration shall not be granted to more than 4 persons in respect of the same property."
-- A PROHIBITION, encoded as the state of affairs the subsection forbids, not
-- as a permission with a ceiling. The burden sits where s 6(1) puts it: the
-- grant is bad if it is to more than 4, and this decision is TRUE exactly then.
--
-- "in respect of the same property" is carried by the grant itself: a grant
-- `limited to a part only of the testator’s estate` names its part, so two
-- grants of three grantees each over different parts do not aggregate. This
-- decision therefore counts the grantees of ONE grant and nothing else.
GIVEN g IS A Grant
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 6(1) — granted to more than 4 persons in respect of the same property` g MEANS
    count (g's `the grantees`) GREATER THAN 4

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 6(2) — "if there is a minority"; s 6(3)
-- JOIN — GUARDIANSHIP OF INFANTS ACT 1934. "a minority" is infancy, and no one
-- of these four Acts defines an infant; the age of majority comes from outside
-- them and arrives on `Person` as `the date on which the person attains the age
-- of majority`. The same fact is the whole subject of the Guardianship of
-- Infants Act and the trigger of s 21 below. FOUR provisions of this Act ask
-- it: s 6(2) here, s 21(1)-(3), s 29(8)(a) and s 63(1). The closing section
-- counts them as ONE join seen from four places.
--
-- AMBIGUITY A4 — whose minority. s 6(2) says only "if there is a minority".
--   Reading (i): a minority among anyone concerned in the estate — an infant
--     beneficiary, but also an infant executor or an infant otherwise entitled
--     to a grant.
--   Reading (ii): a minority among the BENEFICIARIES only, on the footing that
--     the mischief is an infant's property being administered by one person,
--     and s 6(4) speaks expressly of "the minority of a beneficiary".
-- TAKEN: reading (i), which is what `the infants concerned in the estate`
-- holds. s 6(2) is drafted without the qualifier that s 6(4) has two
-- subsections later, and the difference is not obviously accidental; reading
-- (i) is also the wider protection. A case file that wants reading (ii) states
-- only the infant beneficiaries in that list.
--
-- Read as at a DATE, because a minority ends. See AMBIGUITY A2 for what an
-- infant whose date of majority is not in evidence does here.
GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`, `the date` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 6(2) — there is a minority` d `the date` MEANS
    any (GIVEN i YIELD ((i's `the infant`) `is an infant on` `the date`))
        (d's `the infants concerned in the estate`)

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 6(2) — "or if a life interest arises under the will"
-- s 6(2) asks only whether one ARISES, so the test is that the will names one;
-- whether it still SUBSISTS is s 6(4)'s different question, below.
GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 6(2) — a life interest arises under the will` d MEANS
    CONSIDER d's `the will`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN FALSE
    WHEN JUST w  THEN NOT (null (w's `the life interests arising under the will`))

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 6(2)
GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`, `the date` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 6(2) — the requirement as to the grantees is engaged` d `the date` MEANS
        "if there is a minority or if a life interest arises under the will,"
    ..  `s 6(2) — there is a minority` d `the date`
    OR  `s 6(2) — a life interest arises under the will` d

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 6(2) — "be granted either to a trust corporation, with or without an individual, or to not less than 2 individuals"; s 6(6)
-- "with or without an individual" is why the first limb does NOT count: one
-- trust corporation alone satisfies s 6(2), and so does a trust corporation
-- beside one individual. Only the second limb counts, and it counts
-- individuals, which is why `Person or corporation` has to distinguish them.
GIVEN g IS A Grant
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 6(2) — granted either to a trust corporation, with or without an individual, or to not less than 2 individuals` g MEANS
        "either to a trust corporation, with or without an individual,"
    ..  any (GIVEN gr YIELD `s 6(6) — a trust corporation` (gr's `the grantee`)) (g's `the grantees`)
    OR  count (filter (GIVEN gr YIELD `an individual, within the meaning of section 6(2)` (gr's `the grantee`))
                      (g's `the grantees`))
            AT LEAST 2

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 6(2)
-- AMBIGUITY A5 — which grants s 6(2) governs. s 2 defines "letters of
-- administration" and "letters of administration with the will annexed" as two
-- separate terms, and s 6(2) names only the first.
--   Reading (i): s 6(2) reaches both, because its own second limb ("if a life
--     interest arises under the will") presupposes a will, and a will case is
--     the will-annexed case.
--   Reading (ii): s 6(2) reaches only letters of administration simpliciter,
--     because s 21(2) shows that the Act says "with or without the will
--     annexed" in as many words when it means both.
-- TAKEN: reading (i). Reading (ii) would leave the life-interest limb with no
-- work to do at all, which is a reading that empties half the subsection.
GIVEN g IS A Grant
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 6(2) — the grant is one of the grants section 6(2) governs` g MEANS
        g's `the kind of grant` EQUALS `letters of administration`
    OR  g's `the kind of grant` EQUALS `letters of administration with the will annexed`

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 6(2)
-- The compliance test, in the implication shape a conditional requirement
-- takes: vacuously satisfied where the requirement is not engaged.
GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`, g IS A Grant, `the date` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 6(2) — the grant complies with section 6(2)` d g `the date` MEANS
        NOT (    `s 6(2) — the grant is one of the grants section 6(2) governs` g
             AND `s 6(2) — the requirement as to the grantees is engaged` d `the date`)
    OR  `s 6(2) — granted either to a trust corporation, with or without an individual, or to not less than 2 individuals` g

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 6(3) — "The court in granting letters of administration may act on such prima facie evidence, furnished by the applicant or any other person, as to whether or not there is a minority or life interest, as may be prescribed by rules made under any written law for the time being in force relating to the courts."
-- NOT ENCODED, and carried inert so a reader can see that it was read and
-- declined rather than missed. s 6(3) is an EVIDENTIAL provision with no
-- determinate output: it permits the court to act on whatever prima facie
-- evidence rules of court prescribe, and this module has neither the rules nor
-- a standard of proof. It is nevertheless load-bearing here — it is the reason
-- AMBIGUITY A2 can be resolved in favour of "not established" without loss.
`s 6(3) — prima facie evidence of a minority or life interest (not encoded)` MEANS
    "The court in granting letters of administration may act on such prima facie evidence, furnished by the applicant or any other person, as to whether or not there is a minority or life interest, as may be prescribed by rules made under any written law for the time being in force relating to the courts."

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 6(4) — "during the minority of a beneficiary or the subsistence of a life interest and until the estate is fully administered"
-- SUBSISTENCE, not arising: a life interest ends with the life tenant, which is
-- why `Life interest` names him. Contrast `s 6(2) — a life interest arises
-- under the will` above, which asks only whether the will creates one.
GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 6(4) — a life interest subsists` d MEANS
    CONSIDER d's `the will`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN FALSE
    WHEN JUST w  THEN any (GIVEN li YIELD NOT (`has died` (li's `the life tenant`)))
                          (w's `the life interests arising under the will`)

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 6(4) — "during the minority of a beneficiary"; s 6(2) — "if there is a minority"
-- s 6(4)'s MINORITY IS NARROWER THAN s 6(2)'s, AND IT IS GIVEN ITS OWN
-- PREDICATE. s 6(2) says only "if there is a minority"; s 6(4) says "during the
-- minority of a beneficiary". AMBIGUITY A4 rules on s 6(2)'s unqualified words
-- and takes the wide reading — anyone concerned in the estate — expressly
-- BECAUSE, as A4 puts it, s 6(2) is drafted without the qualifier that s 6(4)
-- has two subsections later. That reasoning cannot then be carried into
-- s 6(4), whose words ARE the qualifier. An earlier draft called `s 6(2) — there is a
-- minority` here, so an infant EXECUTOR with no interest in the estate opened
-- the s 6(4) window; the inert string beside it quoted the narrow words while
-- the active node applied the wide test.
--
-- DOMAIN GAP — WHO IS A BENEFICIARY. `Infant` carries no beneficiary field, so
-- the fact is computed. Under a will it is exact: a BENEFICIAL disposition to
-- the person, or a life interest of which he is the life tenant.
--
-- THE `beneficial` FILTER IS THE SAME ONE s 13(2)(c) AND s 13(2)(d) APPLY, and
-- it is applied here for the same reason: a charge or direction for the payment
-- of a debt is a disposition made by the will and is NOT a benefit under it.
-- Wills Act 1838 s 10(1) says so in terms, sparing an attesting witness's gift
-- where what he takes is "other than and except charges and directions for the
-- payment of any debt". An earlier draft of THIS decision read every
-- disposition, so an infant whose only interest under the will was such a
-- charge opened the s 6(4) window while standing — correctly, on the s 13(2)(c)
-- rule three hundred lines below — in no rung of s 13(2) at all. One file
-- cannot hold two readings of "beneficiary", and the narrow one is the one the
-- word carries. Asserted on a minimal pair below: the same infant, the same
-- $20,000, differing only in `beneficial`.
--
-- On an intestacy this Act
-- names no distributees at all — that is the Intestate Succession Act's
-- subject — so the nearest class this module holds is s 18(4)(a)'s, which
-- under AMBIGUITY A3 is that Act's classes taken FLAT. Flat over-includes: a
-- remoter relative who would not in fact take still counts here. The
-- over-inclusion WIDENS the window in which the court may appoint a second
-- representative, which is the protective direction for a subsection that
-- exists to stop an infant's property being administered by one person alone.
GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`, p IS A Person
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 6(4) — this person is a beneficiary` d p MEANS
    CONSIDER d's `the will`
    WHEN JUST w  THEN
             NOT (null (filter (GIVEN dp YIELD dp's `beneficial`)
                               (`the dispositions to this person` d (p's `the reference for this person`))))
         OR  any (GIVEN li YIELD (li's `the life tenant`'s `the reference for this person`)
                                 EQUALS (p's `the reference for this person`))
                 (w's `the life interests arising under the will`)
    WHEN NOTHING THEN
             `s 18(4)(a) — one of the husband or widow or next of kin` d (p's `the reference for this person`)

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 6(4) — "during the minority of a beneficiary"
GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`, `the date` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 6(4) — there is a minority of a beneficiary` d `the date` MEANS
    any (GIVEN i YIELD     ((i's `the infant`) `is an infant on` `the date`)
                       AND `s 6(4) — this person is a beneficiary` d (i's `the infant`))
        (d's `the infants concerned in the estate`)

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 6(4)
-- THE ANTECEDENT ONLY. The consequent — "the court may ... appoint one or more
-- personal representatives in addition to the existing personal
-- representative" — is a discretion, and who applies for it ("any person
-- interested or ... the guardian, committee or receiver of any such person")
-- is not a determinate output either. What IS determinate is the WINDOW: one
-- representative, not a trust corporation, a subsisting minority of a
-- beneficiary or a subsisting life interest, and an estate not yet fully
-- administered.
--
-- "only one personal representative" IS COUNTED OVER THE LIVING ONES, for the
-- reason set out at `the personal representatives of the estate still living`:
-- s 25(1) makes representation accrue to the survivor, so an estate whose two
-- administrators are now one is an estate with one.
GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`, `the date` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 6(4) — the window in which the court may appoint an additional personal representative` d `the date` MEANS
        "If there is only one personal representative (not being a trust corporation)"
    ... count (`the personal representatives of the estate still living` d) EQUALS 1
    ... all (GIVEN gr YIELD NOT (`s 6(6) — a trust corporation` (gr's `the grantee`)))
            (`the personal representatives of the estate still living` d)
    ... "then, during the minority of a beneficiary or the subsistence of a life interest"
    ... (   `s 6(4) — there is a minority of a beneficiary` d `the date`
         OR `s 6(4) — a life interest subsists` d)
    ... "and until the estate is fully administered,"
    AND NOT (d's `the estate`'s `fully administered`)

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 6(5) — "This section shall apply to grants made after 1 January 1935 whether the testator or intestate died before or after that date."
-- The date of DEATH is deliberately not read: the closing words exist to say
-- that it does not matter, so a rule that consulted it would contradict them.
GIVEN g IS A Grant
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 6(5) — this section applies to the grant` g MEANS
    CONSIDER g's `the date of the grant`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN FALSE
    WHEN JUST dg THEN dg `is after` (YMD 1935 1 1)


§§ `Section 8 — Grant of probate`

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 8(1) — "Probate may be granted to any executor appointed by a will."; s 8(2) — "The appointment may be express or implied."
-- s 8(2) is a definition and lives in the ontology as `Mode of appointment`,
-- so a grant to an impliedly appointed executor needs no separate rule here.
-- s 8(1) is a bare permission over a class, so the decision is membership of
-- the class, matched by the person's reference.
GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`, `the reference` IS A STRING
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 8(1) — probate may be granted to this person as an executor appointed by a will` d `the reference` MEANS
    CONSIDER d's `the will`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN FALSE
    WHEN JUST w  THEN any (GIVEN x YIELD (x's `the executor`'s `the reference for this person`) EQUALS `the reference`)
                          (w's `the executors appointed by the will`)

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 8(3) — "Subject to section 6, where more than one person is appointed an executor by a will, probate may be granted to one or more of the persons so appointed, without prejudice to a subsequent application by another or others of them for further grant or grants."
-- DOMAIN GAP — A CORPORATE EXECUTOR CANNOT BE STATED. `Executor appointed by a
-- will` holds `the executor IS A Person`, so a trust company appointed
-- executor by a will has no home in the shared ontology, and the grantee test
-- below (which matches by a person's reference) will not find one. This Act
-- plainly contemplates the case: s 28(2) excuses the oath "where the grantee
-- is the Public Trustee or a trust company", and s 2 defines probate as
-- authorising "the executor or executors expressly or impliedly appointed by a
-- testator’s will". Reported rather than worked around; the fix is for
-- `Executor appointed by a will` to hold a `Person or corporation`.
-- The operative content of s 8(3) is the SUBJECTION: probate to a subset of
-- the appointed executors is permitted, but s 6 still binds, so the count in
-- s 6(1) is the real constraint. "without prejudice to a subsequent
-- application" creates no test — it preserves a right that s 8(1) already
-- gives — so it rides inert.
--
-- THE OPENING WORDS ARE AN ANTECEDENT AND ARE TESTED: "where more than one
-- person is appointed an executor by a will". A will appointing a single executor is s 8(1)'s
-- case and not s 8(3)'s: there is no subset to grant to and no "another or
-- others of them" to reserve a right for. An earlier draft quoted these words
-- in the inert string and tested only the two conjuncts either side of them,
-- so the decision answered TRUE of a sole-executor will — a subsection
-- reporting itself satisfied where its own opening words do not reach.
GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`, g IS A Grant
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 8(3) — probate may be granted to one or more of the persons so appointed, subject to section 6` d g MEANS
        "Subject to section 6,"
    ... NOT (`s 6(1) — granted to more than 4 persons in respect of the same property` g)
    ... "where more than one person is appointed an executor by a will,"
    ... count (`the executors appointed by the will of the deceased` d) GREATER THAN 1
    ... "probate may be granted to one or more of the persons so appointed,"
    ... all (GIVEN gr YIELD `s 8(1) — probate may be granted to this person as an executor appointed by a will`
                                d (`the reference of the grantee` gr))
            (g's `the grantees`)
    AND "without prejudice to a subsequent application by another or others of them for further grant or grants."

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 8(3), s 30
-- DOMAIN GAP — A CORPORATION HAS NO REFERENCE TO MATCH ON. Identity in this
-- module is a reference string, and `Person or corporation` carries one only on
-- its `an individual` member; a licensed trust company carries a name and
-- nothing else. So this decision returns the empty string for a corporate
-- grantee, and every reference-matched question answers FALSE of it. Two
-- provisions feel it: s 8(3), where the corporate-executor gap above already
-- reports the same shortfall, and s 30, where a TRUST COMPANY that is a
-- creditor of the deceased and takes letters of administration escapes the
-- creditor's bond entirely, because `s 13(2)(e)` cannot recognise it. `Creditor`
-- can hold a corporate creditor, so the case is expressible in the ontology and
-- only the matching fails. The fix is a reference field on `Person or
-- corporation` rather than on `Person`. Reported rather than papered over with
-- a name comparison, which would make two companies of the same name one.
GIVEN gr IS A Grantee
GIVETH A STRING
`the reference of the grantee` gr MEANS
    CONSIDER `the individual in` (gr's `the grantee`)
    WHEN JUST p  THEN p's `the reference for this person`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN ""


§§ `Sections 9 to 11 — where the will is lost, destroyed, or proved abroad`

-- THE FACTS THESE THREE SECTIONS TURN ON ARE EVIDENTIAL AND ARE NOT IN THE
-- SHARED ONTOLOGY, deliberately: `family-domain.l4` records whether there IS a
-- will, not what has become of the paper. `Deceased person`'s note says so in
-- terms. They therefore arrive here as GIVEN parameters — proved facts, one
-- per limb — rather than as fields on a record. Each section's OUTPUT is a
-- total enum naming the absent case, because a member that names the absence
-- for that section is a real answer and hiding it in a MAYBE would lose it.

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 9(a)-(b)
DECLARE `The probate that may be granted where the will cannot be produced` IS ONE OF
    `no probate may be granted under section 9`
    `probate of that copy or draft, limited until the original is admitted to probate`
    `probate of the contents of the will, limited until the original is admitted to probate`

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 9 — "Where a will has been lost or mislaid after the death of the testator, or where a will cannot for any sufficient reason be produced — (a) if a copy or draft thereof is produced, and it appears that the copy or draft is identical in terms with the original, probate may be granted of that copy or draft, limited until the original is admitted to probate; or (b) if no such copy or draft is produced, probate may be granted of the contents of the will, if they can be sufficiently established, limited as above described."
-- (b) IS GATED ON (a) FAILING, in the statute's own words: "if no such copy or
-- draft is produced". So the two limbs are ordered, not merely disjoint, and a
-- BRANCH is the right shape. "limited as above described" in (b) refers back to
-- (a)'s limitation, which is why both members carry the same closing words.
GIVEN `a will has been lost or mislaid after the death of the testator` IS A BOOLEAN
      `a will cannot for any sufficient reason be produced` IS A BOOLEAN
      `a copy or draft thereof is produced` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it appears that the copy or draft is identical in terms with the original` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the contents of the will can be sufficiently established` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `The probate that may be granted where the will cannot be produced`
`s 9 — probate of copy or draft, or of contents` MEANS
    BRANCH IF NOT (   `a will has been lost or mislaid after the death of the testator`
                   OR `a will cannot for any sufficient reason be produced`)
              THEN `no probate may be granted under section 9`
           IF     `a copy or draft thereof is produced`
              AND `it appears that the copy or draft is identical in terms with the original`
              THEN `probate of that copy or draft, limited until the original is admitted to probate`
           IF     NOT `a copy or draft thereof is produced`
              AND `the contents of the will can be sufficiently established`
              THEN `probate of the contents of the will, limited until the original is admitted to probate`
           OTHERWISE `no probate may be granted under section 9`

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 10 — "Where a will has been destroyed, otherwise than by the act or with the consent of the testator, probate may be granted of a copy or draft thereof, or of the contents thereof, if they can be sufficiently established."
-- "otherwise than by the act or with the consent of the testator" is a
-- NEGATIVE limb and is kept as one: a will destroyed BY the testator is a
-- revocation, and s 10 must not reach it.
GIVEN `a will has been destroyed` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the will was destroyed by the act or with the consent of the testator` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the copy, draft or contents can be sufficiently established` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 10 — probate may be granted of a copy or draft thereof, or of the contents thereof` MEANS
        "Where a will has been destroyed,"
    ... `a will has been destroyed`
    ... "otherwise than by the act or with the consent of the testator,"
    ... NOT `the will was destroyed by the act or with the consent of the testator`
    ... "if they can be sufficiently established."
    AND `the copy, draft or contents can be sufficiently established`

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 11 — "When a will has been proved and deposited in a court of competent jurisdiction situated beyond the limits of Singapore, and a properly authenticated copy of the will is produced, probate may be granted of such copy or letters of administration may be granted with a copy of such copy annexed."
-- s 11 offers a CHOICE of two grants on one set of facts, not a ranked pair,
-- so both members are available together and the result is a list rather than
-- a BRANCH. Note that this section is NOT the re-sealing machinery of Part 10:
-- it makes a fresh Singapore grant on the footing of a foreign proving,
-- whereas s 47 gives a foreign grant effect here. Part 10 is out of scope.
GIVEN `a will has been proved and deposited in a court of competent jurisdiction situated beyond the limits of Singapore` IS A BOOLEAN
      `a properly authenticated copy of the will is produced` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A LIST OF `Kind of grant`
`s 11 — administration with copy annexed of authenticated copy of will proved abroad` MEANS
    IF      `a will has been proved and deposited in a court of competent jurisdiction situated beyond the limits of Singapore`
        AND `a properly authenticated copy of the will is produced`
    THEN LIST `probate`, `letters of administration with the will annexed`
    ELSE EMPTY


§§ `Sections 12 and 16 — a codicil propounded after a grant`

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 12(1)-(2)
-- THE TWO SUBSECTIONS DIFFER IN MODALITY AND THE MEMBER NAMES CARRY IT.
-- s 12(1) is PERMISSIVE — "separate probate may be granted of the codicil";
-- s 12(2) is MANDATORY — "the probate shall be revoked, and a new probate
-- shall be granted". Collapsing them into a single propounding boolean
-- boolean would lose exactly that, which is the only thing s 12 decides.
DECLARE `The consequence of a codicil propounded after a grant` IS ONE OF
    `nothing follows under section 12`
    `separate probate may be granted of the codicil`
    `the probate shall be revoked, and a new probate shall be granted of the will and codicil together`

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 12 — "12.—(1) Where, after probate has been granted, a codicil of the will is propounded, separate probate may be granted of the codicil. (2) Where the codicil expressly or impliedly revokes the appointment of any executor to whom probate has been granted, the probate shall be revoked, and a new probate shall be granted of the will and codicil together."
-- s 12(2) is tested FIRST because it is the stronger consequence and it
-- displaces s 12(1): where the codicil revokes the appointment of an executor
-- who holds probate, the answer is not the s 12(1) one but
-- revocation and a fresh grant of both instruments together.
GIVEN `a codicil of the will is propounded after probate has been granted` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the codicil expressly or impliedly revokes the appointment of any executor to whom probate has been granted` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `The consequence of a codicil propounded after a grant`
`s 12 — codicil propounded after probate` MEANS
    BRANCH IF NOT `a codicil of the will is propounded after probate has been granted`
              THEN `nothing follows under section 12`
           IF `the codicil expressly or impliedly revokes the appointment of any executor to whom probate has been granted`
              THEN `the probate shall be revoked, and a new probate shall be granted of the will and codicil together`
           OTHERWISE `separate probate may be granted of the codicil`

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 16 — "Section 12 shall apply in the case of a grant of letters of administration with the will annexed, in like manner as they apply in the case of a grant of probate."
-- s 16 is an APPLICATION provision and adds no test of its own, so it is
-- encoded by calling s 12 rather than by restating it. Encoding it as a
-- separate rule would create two places where s 12's logic lives.
GIVEN `a codicil of the will is propounded after letters of administration with the will annexed have been granted` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the codicil expressly or impliedly revokes the appointment of any executor to whom the grant has been made` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `The consequence of a codicil propounded after a grant`
`s 16 — codicil propounded after grant of letters of administration` MEANS
    `s 12 — codicil propounded after probate`
        `a codicil of the will is propounded after letters of administration with the will annexed have been granted`
        `the codicil expressly or impliedly revokes the appointment of any executor to whom the grant has been made`


§§ `Section 13 — Letters of administration with the will annexed, on failure of executors`

-- AMBIGUITY A10 — the five limbs where the will appoints no executor at all.
--   Reading (i): all five are satisfied. `all` over an empty list is TRUE, and
--     limbs (c) and (e) are negatives over that same empty list, so on the
--     bare words every limb answers YES.
--   Reading (ii): limbs (b) to (e) PRESUPPOSE an executor — "the executor or
--     all the executors appointed by will", "no executor survives", "all the
--     executors die", "the executors ... do not appear" — and are neither true
--     nor false where none was appointed.
-- TAKEN: reading (i), the literal one. Limb (a) is satisfied in exactly that
-- case, and the limbs are disjunctive, so the GATE gives the same answer on
-- either reading; writing a non-emptiness presupposition into (b) to (e) would
-- add words the section does not have to buy nothing. A reader inspecting a
-- single limb should know this, which is why it is marked rather than left to
-- be discovered from a fixture.

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 13(1)(a) — "no executor is appointed by a will"
GIVEN w IS A Will
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 13(1)(a) — no executor is appointed by a will` w MEANS
    null (w's `the executors appointed by the will`)

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 13(1)(b) — "legally incapable of acting as such"; s 21(1); s 22(1)
-- THREE SOURCES OF LEGAL INCAPACITY, and two of them are this Act's own.
-- `family-domain.l4` carries only the RESIDUE — incapacity arising outside
-- ss 21(1) and 22(1) — precisely so that this module computes the other two
-- itself and a case file cannot say that an executor is an infant and
-- simultaneously not legally incapable.
GIVEN x IS AN `Executor appointed by a will`, `the date` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 13(1)(b) — this executor is legally incapable of acting as such` x `the date` MEANS
        x's `legally incapable of acting as such, otherwise than under section 21(1) or section 22(1)`
    ..  ((x's `the executor`) `is an infant on` `the date`)
    OR  x's `the executor`'s `lacks mental capacity (within the meaning of the Mental Capacity Act 2008) to make decisions with respect to the management and administration of the person's property and affairs`

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 13(1)(b) — "the executor or all the executors appointed by will are legally incapable of acting as such, or have renounced the right to act as such"
-- AMBIGUITY A6 — how "or have renounced" distributes.
--   Reading (i): per executor — every appointed executor is disqualified,
--     each by one route or the other, so a will with one incapable executor
--     and one who has renounced satisfies the limb.
--   Reading (ii): per class — either all of them are incapable, or all of
--     them have renounced, and a mixed case does not satisfy it.
-- TAKEN: reading (i). The mischief of s 13(1) is that no appointed executor
-- can take the grant, and reading (ii) would leave a will whose executors are
-- disqualified in different ways with no route to a grant at all — which
-- limb (b) plainly exists to prevent.
GIVEN x IS AN `Executor appointed by a will`, `the date` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 13(1)(b) — this executor is disqualified` x `the date` MEANS
        `s 13(1)(b) — this executor is legally incapable of acting as such` x `the date`
    OR  x's `has renounced the right to act as such`

GIVEN w IS A Will, `the date` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 13(1)(b) — the executors are legally incapable of acting as such, or have renounced the right to act as such` w `the date` MEANS
    all (GIVEN x YIELD `s 13(1)(b) — this executor is disqualified` x `the date`)
        (w's `the executors appointed by the will`)

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 13(1)(c) — "no executor survives the testator"
-- See AMBIGUITY A1 for what an undated death does to survivorship.
GIVEN w IS A Will
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 13(1)(c) — no executor survives the testator` w MEANS
    NOT (any (GIVEN x YIELD ((x's `the executor`) `survives` (w's `the testator`)))
             (w's `the executors appointed by the will`))

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 13(1)(d) — "all the executors die before obtaining probate or before having administered all the estate of the deceased"
-- NEITHER COMPARISON IS WITH THE TESTATOR'S DEATH — that is limb (c)'s. The
-- first is the executor's death against the date of a grant of probate naming
-- him as executor; the second is his death against whether he had by then
-- administered all the estate.
GIVEN gr IS A Grantee, x IS AN `Executor appointed by a will`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`this grantee is this executor, taking as executor` gr x MEANS
        ((gr's `the grantee`) `is the individual whose reference is` (x's `the executor`'s `the reference for this person`))
    AND gr's `the capacity in which the grant is made` EQUALS `as executor`

GIVEN g IS A Grant, x IS AN `Executor appointed by a will`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the grant was made in this executor's lifetime` g x MEANS
    CONSIDER `the date of death of` (x's `the executor`)
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOT (`has died` (x's `the executor`))
    WHEN JUST dx THEN
        CONSIDER g's `the date of the grant`
        WHEN NOTHING THEN FALSE
        WHEN JUST dg THEN NOT (dg `is after` dx)

GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`, x IS AN `Executor appointed by a will`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 13(1)(d) — this executor obtained probate` d x MEANS
    any (GIVEN g YIELD `a grant of probate obtained by this executor in his lifetime` g x)
        (d's `the grants made in respect of the estate`)

GIVEN g IS A Grant, x IS AN `Executor appointed by a will`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a grant of probate obtained by this executor in his lifetime` g x MEANS
        g's `the kind of grant` EQUALS `probate`
    ... NOT (g's `revoked`)
    ... any (GIVEN gr YIELD `this grantee is this executor, taking as executor` gr x) (g's `the grantees`)
    AND `the grant was made in this executor's lifetime` g x

GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`, x IS AN `Executor appointed by a will`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 13(1)(d) — this executor died before obtaining probate or before having administered all the estate of the deceased` d x MEANS
        `has died` (x's `the executor`)
    AND (   NOT (`s 13(1)(d) — this executor obtained probate` d x)
         OR NOT (x's `has administered all the estate of the deceased`))

GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`, w IS A Will
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 13(1)(d) — all the executors die before obtaining probate or before having administered all the estate of the deceased` d w MEANS
    all (GIVEN x YIELD `s 13(1)(d) — this executor died before obtaining probate or before having administered all the estate of the deceased` d x)
        (w's `the executors appointed by the will`)

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 13(1)(e) — "the executors appointed by any will do not appear and extract probate"
GIVEN w IS A Will
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 13(1)(e) — the executors appointed by any will do not appear and extract probate` w MEANS
    all (GIVEN x YIELD NOT (x's `appeared and extracted probate`))
        (w's `the executors appointed by the will`)

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 13(1)
-- THE GATE ONLY. The consequent — "letters of administration with the will
-- annexed may be granted to such person or persons as the court considers the
-- fittest to administer the estate" — is a discretion with no determinate
-- output and is NOT encoded; s 13(2)'s ranked class, below, is the part of
-- that choice the Act does fix.
GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`, w IS A Will, `the date` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 13(1) — letters of administration with the will annexed may be granted on failure of executors` d w `the date` MEANS
        "(a) no executor is appointed by a will;"
    ..  `s 13(1)(a) — no executor is appointed by a will` w
    ..  "(b) the executor or all the executors appointed by will are legally incapable of acting as such, or have renounced the right to act as such;"
    ..  `s 13(1)(b) — the executors are legally incapable of acting as such, or have renounced the right to act as such` w `the date`
    ..  "(c) no executor survives the testator;"
    ..  `s 13(1)(c) — no executor survives the testator` w
    ..  "(d) all the executors die before obtaining probate or before having administered all the estate of the deceased; or"
    ..  `s 13(1)(d) — all the executors die before obtaining probate or before having administered all the estate of the deceased` d w
    ..  "(e) the executors appointed by any will do not appear and extract probate,"
    OR  `s 13(1)(e) — the executors appointed by any will do not appear and extract probate` w

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 13(2)(a)-(e)
-- A RANKED CLASS, and the rungs are the statute's own words. The order of the
-- members IS the order of the subsection — "shall belong to the following
-- persons in the following order" — so a reader can check the ranking by
-- reading down the DECLARE.
DECLARE `The rung of the class having a prior right to a grant under section 13(1)` IS ONE OF
    `a universal or residuary legatee`
    `a legal personal representative of a deceased universal or residuary legatee`
    `such person or persons, being beneficiaries under the will, as would have been entitled to a grant of letters of administration if the deceased had died intestate`
    `a legatee having a beneficial interest`
    `a creditor of the deceased`

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 13(2)(a) — "a universal or residuary legatee"; s 13(2)(d) — "a legatee having a beneficial interest"
GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`, `the reference` IS A STRING
GIVETH A LIST OF Disposition
`the dispositions to this person` d `the reference` MEANS
    CONSIDER d's `the will`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN EMPTY
    WHEN JUST w  THEN filter (GIVEN dp YIELD ((dp's `the donee`) `is the individual whose reference is` `the reference`))
                             (w's `the dispositions made by the will`)

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 13(2)(a)
GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`, `the reference` IS A STRING
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 13(2)(a) — a universal or residuary legatee` d `the reference` MEANS
    any (GIVEN dp YIELD `a universal or residuary gift` dp)
        (`the dispositions to this person` d `the reference`)

GIVEN dp IS A Disposition
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a universal or residuary gift` dp MEANS
        dp's `the kind of disposition` EQUALS `a universal gift`
    OR  dp's `the kind of disposition` EQUALS `a residuary gift`

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 13(2)(d) — "a legatee having a beneficial interest"
-- WIDER THAN (a) AND DELIBERATELY SO: a universal or residuary legatee is also
-- a legatee having a beneficial interest, so a person can answer both, and the
-- BRANCH below is what makes (a) win. The rungs overlap in the statute too;
-- that is what "in the following order" is for.
GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`, `the reference` IS A STRING
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 13(2)(d) — a legatee having a beneficial interest` d `the reference` MEANS
    any (GIVEN dp YIELD dp's `beneficial`)
        (`the dispositions to this person` d `the reference`)

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 13(2)(e) — "a creditor of the deceased"
GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`, `the reference` IS A STRING
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 13(2)(e) — a creditor of the deceased` d `the reference` MEANS
    any (GIVEN c YIELD ((c's `the creditor`) `is the individual whose reference is` `the reference`))
        (d's `the estate`'s `the creditors of the deceased`)

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 13(2)(c) — "such person or persons, being beneficiaries under the will, as would have been entitled to a grant of letters of administration if the deceased had died intestate"; s 18(4)(a)
-- TWO CONJUNCTS AND A COUNTERFACTUAL. The person must (1) be a beneficiary
-- under the will and (2) be someone who WOULD have been entitled to a grant of
-- letters of administration had there been no will. Limb (2) is s 18(4)(a)'s
-- class — "the husband or widow or next of kin or any of them" — read
-- counterfactually, which is why the intestacy of the deceased is not tested:
-- s 13 applies only where there IS a will, so the counterfactual is the whole
-- point of the limb.
--
-- JOIN — INTESTATE SUCCESSION ACT 1967. Who "the next of kin" are is not
-- settled by this Act; see AMBIGUITY A3.
--
-- THE WORDS "being beneficiaries under the will" NEED A BENEFICIAL
-- DISPOSITION, and not merely a disposition. `Disposition` carries `beneficial`
-- as a field of its own precisely because the two come apart: a charge or
-- direction for the payment of a debt is a disposition made by the will and is
-- NOT a benefit under it. Wills Act 1838 s 10(1) says so in terms, sparing an
-- attesting witness's gift where what he takes is "other than and except
-- charges and directions for the payment of any debt". A person whose only
-- interest
-- under the will is such a charge is a creditor, whose place is rung (e), and
-- not a beneficiary, whose place is here. An earlier draft tested only that
-- some disposition ran to him, which put a secured creditor two rungs above
-- the one s 13(2) gives him — and above rung (d), which asks for the same
-- beneficial interest this rung does.
GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`, `the reference` IS A STRING
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 13(2)(c) — a beneficiary under the will who would have been entitled to a grant on intestacy` d `the reference` MEANS
        "being beneficiaries under the will,"
    ... NOT (null (filter (GIVEN dp YIELD dp's `beneficial`)
                          (`the dispositions to this person` d `the reference`)))
    ... "as would have been entitled to a grant of letters of administration if the deceased had died intestate"
    AND `s 18(4)(a) — one of the husband or widow or next of kin` d `the reference`

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 13(2)
-- DOMAIN GAP — limb (b). "a legal personal representative of a deceased
-- universal or residuary legatee" needs an edge from a dead legatee to his own
-- personal representative, and `family-domain.l4` has none: `Grant` is rooted
-- at THIS deceased's estate, so a grant made in the legatee's own estate is
-- outside the model entirely. The fact therefore arrives here as a GIVEN, and
-- is reported as a gap rather than approximated.
--
-- The result is a MAYBE and not a total enum, because s 13(2) does NOT name an
-- absent case: a person who is in none of the five classes simply has no prior
-- right, and inventing a sixth member would put a word in the subsection.
GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`
      `the reference` IS A STRING
      `the person is a legal personal representative of a deceased universal or residuary legatee` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE `The rung of the class having a prior right to a grant under section 13(1)`
`s 13(2) — the rung of the prior-right class in which this person stands` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `s 13(2)(a) — a universal or residuary legatee` d `the reference`
              THEN JUST `a universal or residuary legatee`
           IF `the person is a legal personal representative of a deceased universal or residuary legatee`
              THEN JUST `a legal personal representative of a deceased universal or residuary legatee`
           IF `s 13(2)(c) — a beneficiary under the will who would have been entitled to a grant on intestacy` d `the reference`
              THEN JUST `such person or persons, being beneficiaries under the will, as would have been entitled to a grant of letters of administration if the deceased had died intestate`
           IF `s 13(2)(d) — a legatee having a beneficial interest` d `the reference`
              THEN JUST `a legatee having a beneficial interest`
           IF `s 13(2)(e) — a creditor of the deceased` d `the reference`
              THEN JUST `a creditor of the deceased`
           OTHERWISE NOTHING


§§ `Sections 14, 15, 17, 19 and 20 — grants to attorneys, and limited grants`

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 14 — "and there is no other executor within Singapore willing to act"
GIVEN x IS AN `Executor appointed by a will`, y IS AN `Executor appointed by a will`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`another executor within Singapore willing to act` x y MEANS
        NOT ((y's `the executor`'s `the reference for this person`) EQUALS (x's `the executor`'s `the reference for this person`))
    ... NOT (y's `the executor`'s `absent from Singapore`)
    AND y's `the executor`'s `willing to act`

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 14 — "Where an executor appointed by a will is absent from Singapore, and there is no other executor within Singapore willing to act, letters of administration with the will annexed may be granted to a duly authorised attorney of the absent executor, limited until he obtains probate for himself, and in the meantime to any purpose to which the attorney’s authority is limited."
GIVEN w IS A Will, x IS AN `Executor appointed by a will`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 14 — letters of administration with the will annexed may be granted to a duly authorised attorney of the absent executor` w x MEANS
        "Where an executor appointed by a will is absent from Singapore,"
    ... x's `the executor`'s `absent from Singapore`
    ... "and there is no other executor within Singapore willing to act,"
    AND NOT (any (GIVEN y YIELD `another executor within Singapore willing to act` x y)
                 (w's `the executors appointed by the will`))

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 14 — "limited until he obtains probate for himself, and in the meantime to any purpose to which the attorney’s authority is limited"; s 15 — "limited as described in section 14"
-- THE LIMITATION IS AN OUTPUT, and it is one the ontology can hold: a
-- `limited in some other way` carrying the section's own terms. s 15 borrows
-- it by cross-reference, so both sections produce this one value.
`s 14 — the limitation on a grant to the attorney of an absent executor` MEANS
    `limited in some other way`
        "limited until he obtains probate for himself, and in the meantime to any purpose to which the attorney’s authority is limited"

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 15 — "Where any person to whom letters of administration with the will annexed might be granted under section 13 is absent from Singapore, letters of administration with the will annexed may be granted to his duly authorised attorney, limited as described in section 14."
-- s 15 is PARASITIC ON s 13, and in TWO ways, both of which have to be tested.
-- Its subject is "any person to whom letters of administration with the will
-- annexed might be granted under section 13", which is a description of a
-- PERSON and not only of the estate: so (1) the s 13(1) gate has to be made out
-- before s 15 can be asked at all, and (2) the person himself has to be one to
-- whom such a grant might be made. An earlier draft tested (1) and then only
-- whether `p` was abroad, which let any absent stranger satisfy the section
-- while the inert string above him quoted the limb that was not being tested.
--
-- WHICH PERSONS "might be granted" A GRANT UNDER s 13. s 13(1)'s own consequent
-- is at large — "to such person or persons as the court considers the fittest
-- to administer the estate" — and is not encoded, for the reason given at that
-- site. What s 13 fixes is s 13(2)'s ranked class, "A prior right to a grant
-- under subsection (1)", and that determinate class is what is tested here. A
-- stranger whom the court might in fact think fittest is therefore outside this
-- encoding of s 15, which is the same boundary the s 13(1) consequent draws.
GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`
      w IS A Will
      p IS A Person
      `the person is a legal personal representative of a deceased universal or residuary legatee` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the date` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 15 — letters of administration with the will annexed may be granted to the attorney of an absent person entitled` d w p `the person is a legal personal representative of a deceased universal or residuary legatee` `the date` MEANS
        "Where any person to whom letters of administration with the will annexed might be granted under section 13"
    ... `s 13(1) — letters of administration with the will annexed may be granted on failure of executors` d w `the date`
    ... isJust (`s 13(2) — the rung of the prior-right class in which this person stands`
                    d (p's `the reference for this person`)
                    `the person is a legal personal representative of a deceased universal or residuary legatee`)
    ... "is absent from Singapore,"
    AND p's `absent from Singapore`

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 17 — "When no will of the deceased is forthcoming, but there is reason to believe that there is a will in existence, letters of administration may be granted, limited until the will or an authenticated copy thereof is produced."
-- THE TWO FACTS ARE EVIDENTIAL and are not in the shared ontology; see the
-- note on ss 9-11. Note that s 17's premise is NOT that the deceased died
-- intestate — it is that a will is believed to exist but cannot be produced —
-- so this is not s 18's case even though the grant is of the same kind.
GIVEN `no will of the deceased is forthcoming` IS A BOOLEAN
      `there is reason to believe that there is a will in existence` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 17 — letters of administration may be granted, limited until the will is produced` MEANS
        `no will of the deceased is forthcoming`
    AND `there is reason to believe that there is a will in existence`

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 17 — "limited until the will or an authenticated copy thereof is produced"
`s 17 — the limitation on a grant until the will is produced` MEANS
    `limited in some other way` "limited until the will or an authenticated copy thereof is produced"

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 19 — "Where a person entitled to letters of administration in case of intestacy is absent from Singapore, and no person equally entitled is willing to act, letters of administration may be granted to an authorised attorney of the absent person, limited until he obtains a grant to himself."
-- "no person equally entitled is willing to act" is read over the s 18(4)(a)
-- class, since that is who is entitled to letters of administration in case of
-- intestacy; "equally" is not further defined and, on AMBIGUITY A3's reading,
-- the class is flat, so every other member of it is equally entitled.
GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`, p IS A Person
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 19 — letters of administration may be granted to an authorised attorney of the absent person` d p MEANS
        "Where a person entitled to letters of administration in case of intestacy is absent from Singapore,"
    ... `s 18(4)(a) — one of the husband or widow or next of kin` d (p's `the reference for this person`)
    ... p's `absent from Singapore`
    ... "and no person equally entitled is willing to act,"
    AND NOT (any (GIVEN q YIELD `a person equally entitled who is willing to act` p q)
                 (`the persons entitled under section 18(4)(a)` d))

GIVEN p IS A Person, q IS A Person
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a person equally entitled who is willing to act` p q MEANS
        NOT ((q's `the reference for this person`) EQUALS (p's `the reference for this person`))
    AND q's `willing to act`

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 19 — "limited until he obtains a grant to himself"
`s 19 — the limitation on a grant to the attorney of an absent person entitled` MEANS
    `limited in some other way` "limited until he obtains a grant to himself"

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 20 — "Pending any probate action, letters of administration may be granted to such person as the court may appoint, limited so that the administrator shall not be empowered to distribute the estate, and shall be subject to such control by, and direction of, the court as the court thinks fit."
-- THE PERSON IS AT LARGE ("such person as the court may appoint") and so is
-- the control ("as the court thinks fit"), so neither is encoded. What IS
-- fixed is the LIMITATION, and it falls squarely inside the shared ontology's
-- `a grant that does not permit the distribution of the testator’s estate` —
-- the Wills Act s 28(4)(b) category, into which `family-domain.l4` maps this
-- section expressly.
GIVEN `a probate action is pending` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 20 — letters of administration may be granted pending a probate action` MEANS
    `a probate action is pending`

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 20 — "limited so that the administrator shall not be empowered to distribute the estate"
`s 20 — the limitation on a grant pending a probate action` MEANS
    `a grant that does not permit the distribution of the testator’s estate`


§§ `Section 18 — Letters of administration on intestacy`

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 18(1) — "When a person has died intestate, the court may grant letters of administration of his estate."; s 2
-- AMBIGUITY A7 — what "died intestate" means in this Act. The Probate and
-- Administration Act does NOT define "intestate"; the Intestate Succession Act
-- 1967 does, and inclusively — s 3 of that Act: "intestate" includes any
-- person who leaves a will but dies intestate as to some beneficial interest.
--   Reading (i): for s 18, a person dies intestate when no will is admitted to
--     probate at all, so a partial intestacy is not s 18's case.
--   Reading (ii): the Intestate Succession Act's inclusive definition is
--     carried across, so a will disposing of part engages s 18 as to the rest.
-- TAKEN: reading (i). s 2 of THIS Act defines the two grants by what they
-- authorise — letters of administration authorise administration of "an
-- intestate’s estate in accordance with law", letters with the will annexed
-- authorise it "in compliance with the directions contained in his will" — and
-- an estate with a will has directions to comply with, so it takes the second
-- grant under s 13 and not this one. Reading (ii) is about DISTRIBUTION, which
-- is the Intestate Succession Act's subject and not this Act's; the two Acts
-- can differ here without contradiction, and this module says so rather than
-- borrowing a definition the Act did not adopt.
GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 18(1) — the person has died intestate` d MEANS
    isNothing (d's `the will`)

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 18(2) — "and in regard to land settled previously to the death of the deceased, letters of administration may be granted to the trustees of the settlement"
-- ONLY THE SECOND HALF OF s 18(2) IS ENCODED. The first half — "the court
-- shall have regard to the rights of all persons interested in the estate of
-- the deceased person or the proceeds of sale thereof" — is a direction as to
-- how a discretion is to be exercised and produces no determinate output, so
-- it is carried inert and named in the closing section. The settled-land limb
-- is different: it names a class of grantee, and the ontology can identify
-- them.
--
-- BOTH CONJUNCTS BELOW ARE THIS DECISION'S AND NOT THE SUBSECTION'S, which is
-- the same flag s 18(4) carries two subsections later. s 18(2) says only "in
-- regard to land settled previously to the death of the deceased, letters of
-- administration may be granted to the trustees of the settlement". It does not
-- say that the settled land must be worth something, and it does not say that
-- the settlement must have trustees. The first is this module's proxy for
-- "land settled previously to the death" — the ontology records the settled
-- land as a value and not as a yes-or-no — and the second is a class-occupied
-- gate of the kind s 18(4) uses: a permission to grant to the trustees of the
-- settlement opens no class where the settlement has none. Kept flagged so a
-- reader can see which words are the statute's and which are this decision's.
GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 18(2) — letters of administration may be granted to the trustees of the settlement` d MEANS
        "in regard to land settled previously to the death of the deceased,"
    ... d's `the estate`'s `the value of land settled previously to the death of the deceased` GREATER THAN 0
    ... "letters of administration may be granted to the trustees of the settlement."
    AND NOT (null (d's `the estate`'s `the trustees of the settlement`))

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 18(4)(a) — "letters of administration may be granted to the husband or widow or next of kin or any of them"
-- The class, as a list, because s 18(4)(b) and s 18(4)(d)(i) both COUNT it.
-- The spouse limb and the next-of-kin limb are kept apart in the construction
-- for the reason given on `the next of kin of the deceased`: s 18(4)(a) names
-- them disjunctively and folding one into the other would make the Act's own
-- "or" redundant.
GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A LIST OF Person
`the persons entitled under section 18(4)(a)` d MEANS
    concat (LIST `the husband or widow of the deceased` d
               , `the next of kin of the deceased` d)

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 18(4)(a)
GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`, `the reference` IS A STRING
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 18(4)(a) — one of the husband or widow or next of kin` d `the reference` MEANS
    any (GIVEN p YIELD (p's `the reference for this person`) EQUALS `the reference`)
        (`the persons entitled under section 18(4)(a)` d)

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 18(4)(b) — "when such persons apply for letters of administration, it shall be in the discretion of the court to grant them to any one or more of such persons"
-- THE ANTECEDENT ONLY: whether one of the s 18(4)(a) persons has applied.
-- The consequent is a discretion in terms ("it shall be in the discretion of
-- the court") and is not encoded. The antecedent is nevertheless load-bearing,
-- because s 18(4)(c) is expressly the negative of it.
GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 18(4)(b) — such persons apply for letters of administration` d MEANS
    any (GIVEN a YIELD `an application by one of the section 18(4)(a) persons` d a)
        (d's `the probate applications made in respect of the estate`)

GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`, a IS A `Probate application`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`an application by one of the section 18(4)(a) persons` d a MEANS
    any (GIVEN p YIELD ((a's `the applicant`) `is the individual whose reference is` (p's `the reference for this person`)))
        (`the persons entitled under section 18(4)(a)` d)

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 18(4)(c) — "when no such person applies, letters of administration may be granted to a creditor of the deceased"
GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 18(4)(c) — no such person applies, and letters of administration may be granted to a creditor of the deceased` d MEANS
    NOT (`s 18(4)(b) — such persons apply for letters of administration` d)

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 18(4)(d)(i) — "the next of kin of any person dying intestate, or the greater number of such next of kin, so desire"
-- AMBIGUITY A8 — "the next of kin ... or the greater number of such next of
-- kin, so desire".
--   Reading (i): a MAJORITY — more than half of the next of kin so desire
--     (which the unanimous case satisfies a fortiori, so the first limb of the
--     disjunction adds nothing operative and is read as emphasis).
--   Reading (ii): "the greater number" is the larger of two OPPOSING groups —
--     more of them desire it than oppose it — so next of kin who express no
--     view are not counted in the denominator.
-- TAKEN: reading (i). `Person` carries a single boolean ("so desires ...") and
-- so records no third, abstaining state; on that shape the two readings differ
-- only in the denominator, and the majority reading is the one that cannot be
-- gamed by adding silent relatives. The limb is FALSE where there are no next
-- of kin at all, because "the greater number of such next of kin" presupposes
-- some.
GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 18(4)(d)(i) — the next of kin, or the greater number of such next of kin, so desire` d MEANS
        NOT (null (`the next of kin of the deceased` d))
    AND (   count (filter (GIVEN p YIELD p's `so desires that letters of administration be granted to such person as the court thinks fit`)
                          (`the next of kin of the deceased` d))
              TIMES 2
          GREATER THAN
            count (`the next of kin of the deceased` d))

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 18(4)(d)(ii) — "no next of kin or creditor or other person appears and makes out a claim to letters of administration"
-- NOT the same fact as the applications list being empty, and the ontology
-- keeps them apart deliberately: a person may appear and make out a claim
-- without applying, and may apply and fail to make out a claim.
GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 18(4)(d)(ii) — no next of kin or creditor or other person appears and makes out a claim to letters of administration` d MEANS
    NOT (d's `a next of kin, creditor or other person has appeared and made out a claim to letters of administration`)

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 18(4)(d)
-- A NOTE ON THE SOURCE TEXT. In source/PAA1934.txt the label "(d)" appears
-- TWICE — once opening "in any case where —" and once again after sub-limbs
-- (i) and (ii), opening "letters of administration of the estate and effects
-- of the intestate may be granted ...". The second occurrence is the TAIL of
-- paragraph (d), not a further paragraph; the layout extraction repeated the
-- marker where the printed page carries the hanging indent. Read as one
-- paragraph with two alternative antecedents and one consequent.
GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 18(4)(d) — letters of administration may be granted to such person as the court thinks fit` d MEANS
        "(i) the next of kin of any person dying intestate, or the greater number of such next of kin, so desire; or"
    ..  `s 18(4)(d)(i) — the next of kin, or the greater number of such next of kin, so desire` d
    ..  "(ii) no next of kin or creditor or other person appears and makes out a claim to letters of administration,"
    OR  `s 18(4)(d)(ii) — no next of kin or creditor or other person appears and makes out a claim to letters of administration` d

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 18(4)(a), s 18(4)(c), s 18(4)(d)
-- EACH LIMB IS GATED ON ITS CLASS BEING OCCUPIED, which is a property of THIS
-- decision and not of the limb predicates above it. `s 18(4)(c)` states the
-- subsection's own condition — "when no such person applies" — and stops
-- there, because that is all (c) says; but a permission to grant "to a creditor
-- of the deceased" opens no class where the estate has no creditor, and this
-- decision reports the classes that are actually open. Limb (a) is gated the
-- same way. The two are kept apart so that a reader can see which words are the
-- statute's and which are this decision's.
--
-- THE CLASSES s 18(4) OPENS, AS A LIST AND NOT AS A LADDER. More than one may
-- be open at once: (a) and (d) can both be available on the same facts, and
-- (c) is expressly the negative of (b) rather than a rung below (a). A BRANCH
-- returning a single "highest" class would assert a priority the subsection
-- does not state — contrast s 13(2), which says "in the following order" in
-- terms and therefore IS a ladder.
DECLARE `A class to which letters of administration may be granted under section 18(4)` IS ONE OF
    `the husband or widow or next of kin or any of them`
    `a creditor of the deceased, no such person having applied`
    `such person as the court thinks fit`

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 18(4)
GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A LIST OF `A class to which letters of administration may be granted under section 18(4)`
`s 18(4) — the classes to which letters of administration may be granted` d MEANS
    concat
      (LIST (IF NOT (null (`the persons entitled under section 18(4)(a)` d))
             THEN LIST `the husband or widow or next of kin or any of them`
             ELSE EMPTY)
          , (IF     `s 18(4)(c) — no such person applies, and letters of administration may be granted to a creditor of the deceased` d
                AND NOT (null (d's `the estate`'s `the creditors of the deceased`))
             THEN LIST `a creditor of the deceased, no such person having applied`
             ELSE EMPTY)
          , (IF `s 18(4)(d) — letters of administration may be granted to such person as the court thinks fit` d
             THEN LIST `such person as the court thinks fit`
             ELSE EMPTY))

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 18(5) — "Nothing in this section shall affect any law by which special provision is made regarding the estates of persons of a particular religion or race."
-- A SAVING WHOSE OPERAND IS OUTSIDE THIS ACT. s 18(5) does not say what the
-- special provision is or which law makes it; it disapplies s 18 to the extent
-- of whatever such law exists. The class of such laws is not enumerable here,
-- so what is encoded is the narrow case the shared ontology puts in evidence —
-- the deceased being a Muslim, which is also the Intestate Succession Act's
-- s 2 gate — and the wider saving is stated inert beside it. The two are not
-- the same: the Intestate Succession Act s 2 SWITCHES OFF that whole Act,
-- while this subsection merely leaves the other law unaffected.
GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 18(5) — a law making special provision regarding the estates of persons of a particular religion or race is in evidence` d MEANS
        "Nothing in this section shall affect any law by which special provision is made regarding the estates of persons of a particular religion or race."
    AND d's `a Muslim`


§§ `Section 21 — Infants`

-- JOIN — GUARDIANSHIP OF INFANTS ACT 1934, in both directions. s 21(1)'s bar
-- and s 21(2)'s route both turn on infancy, which is the Guardianship Act's
-- entire subject; and s 21(2) grants to "the guardian of the person and
-- property of the infant", who is an office THAT Act creates (its ss 6, 7 and
-- 10) and this one does not. This module can say WHETHER a guardian may be
-- granted to; it cannot say WHO the guardian is.

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 21(1) — "No probate or letters of administration shall be granted to a person while he is an infant."
-- A PROHIBITION, and encoded as one: TRUE exactly when the bar bites. The
-- SHAPE is faithful — the decision is named for the bar and is true when the
-- bar applies, so a caller reads a prohibition and not a permission.
--
-- THE SHAPE IS NOT THE BURDEN, and an earlier draft of this note said it was:
-- "The burden is on the person propounding the grant to show the grantee is
-- not an infant, and a permissive phrasing conditioned on full age would move
-- it." The second half of that is right and the first half is not. Where the
-- age is not in evidence, AMBIGUITY A2 folds NOTHING to FALSE and this bar
-- does NOT bite — so an unproved age passes, which is exactly what a burden on
-- the propounder would prevent. On A2's reasoning that is deliberate: s 6(3)
-- routes the evidential question to the court and to rules of court, and this
-- module declines to answer it from silence. But the encoding leaves the
-- burden where the Act's own forum will put it, and does not itself impose one.
-- See A2 for the argument, for what the fold costs, and for the two
-- neighbouring Acts that answer the same evidential question differently.
GIVEN p IS A Person, `the date` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 21(1) — no probate or letters of administration shall be granted to a person while he is an infant` p `the date` MEANS
    p `is an infant on` `the date`

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 21(2) — "Where an infant would, but for his infancy, be entitled to probate or letters of administration"
-- THE COUNTERFACTUAL IS COMPUTABLE, and this is where s 21 pays for the join.
-- An infant "would, but for his infancy, be entitled" if he is an executor
-- appointed by the will (s 8(1) would give him probate) or if he is within the
-- s 18(4)(a) class (letters of administration would be open to him). Taking it
-- as an unanalysed input would have thrown away the two rules that answer it.
GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`, i IS AN Infant, `the date` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 21(2) — this infant would, but for his infancy, be entitled to probate or letters of administration` d i `the date` MEANS
        ((i's `the infant`) `is an infant on` `the date`)
    AND (   `s 8(1) — probate may be granted to this person as an executor appointed by a will` d (i's `the infant`'s `the reference for this person`)
         OR `s 18(4)(a) — one of the husband or widow or next of kin` d (i's `the infant`'s `the reference for this person`))

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 21(2)-(3)
GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`, `the date` IS A DATE
GIVETH A LIST OF Infant
`the infant executors or persons so entitled` d `the date` MEANS
    filter (GIVEN i YIELD `s 21(2) — this infant would, but for his infancy, be entitled to probate or letters of administration` d i `the date`)
           (d's `the infants concerned in the estate`)

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 10 — "The court may remove from his guardianship any guardian, and may appoint another guardian in his place."; Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 21(2)
-- ***** A JOIN TO THE GUARDIANSHIP OF INFANTS ACT 1934 *****
--
-- s 21(2)'s grantee is "the guardian of the person and property of the
-- infant", and this Act neither creates that office nor says who holds it.
-- The shared ontology carries the answer already construed, on `Infant`'s
-- `the guardians of the infant`, and this module READS it — the same treatment
-- it gives the age of majority at `is an infant on`. Reading an
-- already-construed fact is not deciding it; the closing section's statement
-- that this module "can decide WHETHER a grant may be made to the infant's
-- guardian and cannot decide WHO that guardian is" is discharged by reading
-- the field rather than by leaving the field unread.
--
-- WHAT IS TESTED, AND WHAT IS NOT.
--   The KIND is tested. s 21(2) names "the guardian of the person and property
--   of the infant" — the conjunction is the operative word — and
--   `Kind of guardianship` has three members. A guardian of the person only,
--   or of the property only, is not the person s 21(2) names. This is the one
--   place in this module where that distinction bites.
--   REMOVAL is tested: Guardianship of Infants Act s 10 lets the court "remove
--   from his guardianship any guardian", and a guardian so removed is not the
--   guardian of the infant. The ontology records removal precisely because a
--   removed guardian would otherwise sit in the list indistinguishable from a
--   serving one.
--   HOW HE WAS APPOINTED is NOT tested, and deliberately. s 21(2) asks only
--   who the guardian IS. Which of the Guardianship Act's routes put him there
--   — its ss 6, 7 or 10, or a will under its s 7(1)-(2) and Wills Act s 27(3)
--   — is that Act's question and this one takes the answer as given.
GIVEN gr IS A Grantee, gu IS A Guardian
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
gr `is this guardian of the person and property of the infant` gu MEANS
        (gu's `of the person or of the property`) EQUALS `guardian of the person and property of the infant`
    AND NOT (gu's `removed from his guardianship by the court`)
    AND ((gr's `the grantee`) `is the individual whose reference is` (gu's `the guardian`'s `the reference for this person`))

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 21(2) — "letters of administration with or without the will annexed may, subject to section 6(1), be granted to the guardian of the person and property of the infant, or to such person as the court thinks fit, limited until the infant obtains a grant to himself"
-- ONE GRANTEE, MATCHED AGAINST ONE INFANT, and the match has to run through
-- the grant rather than round it. `Grantee` carries the capacity in which the
-- grant was made and, for that capacity, a pointer to "the person for whose
-- use and benefit the grant is made"; the infant on the far end of that
-- pointer must be one of the infants s 21(2) opens the route for, and this
-- grantee must be that infant's guardian. s 29(8)(a) reads the same pointer
-- the same way, which is the shape this follows.
GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`, gr IS A Grantee, `the date` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 21(2) — this grant is made to the guardian of the person and property of an infant so entitled` d gr `the date` MEANS
        (gr's `the capacity in which the grant is made`) EQUALS `as the guardian of the person and property of the infant`
    AND (CONSIDER gr's `the reference for the person for whose use and benefit the grant is made`
         WHEN NOTHING THEN FALSE
         WHEN JUST r  THEN
             any (GIVEN i YIELD     (i's `the infant`'s `the reference for this person`) EQUALS r
                                AND `s 21(2) — this infant would, but for his infancy, be entitled to probate or letters of administration` d i `the date`
                                AND any (GIVEN gu YIELD gr `is this guardian of the person and property of the infant` gu)
                                        (i's `the guardians of the infant`))
                 (d's `the infants concerned in the estate`))

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 21(2) — "letters of administration with or without the will annexed may, subject to section 6(1), be granted to the guardian of the person and property of the infant, or to such person as the court thinks fit, limited until the infant obtains a grant to himself"
-- FOUR THINGS THE SUBSECTION FIXES AND ONE IT DOES NOT. It fixes the KIND of
-- grant ("with or without the will annexed"), the SUBJECTION to s 6(1), the
-- GRANTEE ("the guardian of the person and property of the infant"), and the
-- LIMITATION ("limited until the infant obtains a grant to himself"). It
-- leaves the person at large in the alternative — "or to such person as the
-- court thinks fit" — which is not encoded; this decision therefore answers
-- the guardian limb only, and a FALSE from it is not a statement that the
-- grant is unlawful, only that it is not the grant to the guardian that the
-- first limb authorises.
--
-- AN EARLIER DRAFT ENCODED THE FIRST TWO AND NOT THE OTHER TWO, while the
-- comment above it counted the limitation among the things the subsection
-- fixes. The rule then answered TRUE for a grant `not limited`, TRUE for a
-- grant `limited to settled land or to trust property`, and TRUE for a grant
-- to a stranger in his own name — reporting a permission for grants s 21(2)
-- does not authorise, on a decision whose own name promises a test of the
-- grantee. The limitation was already computed by
-- `s 21 — the limitation on a grant made under subsection (2)` at the foot of
-- this subsection's block, and simply never conjoined.
--
-- `any` OVER THE GRANTEES, NOT `all`. s 6(1) allows up to four, and the
-- alternative limb lets the court add "such person as the court thinks fit"
-- beside the guardian. What the first limb requires is that the guardian be
-- among the grantees, not that he be alone.
--
-- THE LIMITATION IS COMPARED AGAINST THE COMPUTED ONE, so s 21(3) is live
-- here: with one infant so entitled the grant must run "until the infant
-- obtains a grant to himself" and with two or more "until any of them obtains
-- a grant", and a grant bearing the wrong one of those two is not a grant made
-- under this subsection. That is s 21(3)'s whole operation and it would be
-- inert if the limitation were not read.
GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`, g IS A Grant, `the date` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 21(2) — letters of administration may be granted to the guardian of the person and property of the infant` d g `the date` MEANS
        "Where an infant would, but for his infancy, be entitled to probate or letters of administration,"
    ... NOT (null (`the infant executors or persons so entitled` d `the date`))
    ... "letters of administration with or without the will annexed may, subject to section 6(1), be granted"
    ... (   g's `the kind of grant` EQUALS `letters of administration`
         OR g's `the kind of grant` EQUALS `letters of administration with the will annexed`)
    ... NOT (`s 6(1) — granted to more than 4 persons in respect of the same property` g)
    ... "to the guardian of the person and property of the infant,"
    ... any (GIVEN gr YIELD `s 21(2) — this grant is made to the guardian of the person and property of an infant so entitled` d gr `the date`)
            (g's `the grantees`)
    ... "limited until the infant obtains a grant to himself."
    AND (g's `the limitation`) EQUALS (`s 21 — the limitation on a grant made under subsection (2)` d `the date`)

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 21(3) — "Where there are 2 or more infant executors or persons so entitled, any grant made under subsection (2) shall be limited until any of them obtains a grant."
GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`, `the date` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 21(3) — there are 2 or more infant executors or persons so entitled` d `the date` MEANS
    count (`the infant executors or persons so entitled` d `the date`) AT LEAST 2

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 21(2) — "limited until the infant obtains a grant to himself"; s 21(3) — "shall be limited until any of them obtains a grant"
-- s 21(3) DISPLACES s 21(2)'s LIMITATION, and that displacement is the only
-- thing s 21(3) does. With one infant the grant ends when THAT infant takes a
-- grant; with two or more it ends when ANY of them does, which is earlier. A
-- module that recorded only "a limited grant" would lose the difference, and
-- the difference is when the grantee's authority stops.
GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`, `the date` IS A DATE
GIVETH A `Limitation on the grant`
`s 21 — the limitation on a grant made under subsection (2)` d `the date` MEANS
    IF   `s 21(3) — there are 2 or more infant executors or persons so entitled` d `the date`
    THEN `limited in some other way` "limited until any of them obtains a grant"
    ELSE `limited in some other way` "limited until the infant obtains a grant to himself"


§§ `Section 22 — Persons who lack mental capacity`

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 22(1) — "No probate or letters of administration are to be granted to a person who lacks mental capacity (within the meaning of the Mental Capacity Act 2008) to make decisions with respect to the management and administration of the person’s property and affairs."
-- ALREADY-CONSTRUED INPUT. s 22(1) imports the Mental Capacity Act 2008
-- wholesale and this cleanroom does not encode that Act, so the fact arrives
-- proved. What this module does own is that the bar is a PROHIBITION and that
-- it is one of the two sources of s 13(1)(b)’s "legally incapable of acting
-- as such" that this module computes rather than takes as given.
GIVEN p IS A Person
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 22(1) — no probate or letters of administration are to be granted to a person who lacks mental capacity` p MEANS
    p's `lacks mental capacity (within the meaning of the Mental Capacity Act 2008) to make decisions with respect to the management and administration of the person's property and affairs`

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 22(2) — "Where any such person, if he or she did not lack mental capacity, would be entitled to probate or letters of administration, letters of administration with or without the will annexed may be granted to — (a) the person to whom the care of the estate of the person who lacks mental capacity has been lawfully committed; or (b) any other person that the court thinks fit,"
-- THE SAME COUNTERFACTUAL SHAPE AS s 21(2), and computed the same way. Limb
-- (b) ("any other person that the court thinks fit") is a discretion and is
-- not encoded; limb (a) IS a determinate class, and the ontology names it as a
-- capacity in which a grant may be made.
GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`, p IS A Person
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 22(2) — this person would, if he or she did not lack mental capacity, be entitled to probate or letters of administration` d p MEANS
        `s 22(1) — no probate or letters of administration are to be granted to a person who lacks mental capacity` p
    AND (   `s 8(1) — probate may be granted to this person as an executor appointed by a will` d (p's `the reference for this person`)
         OR `s 18(4)(a) — one of the husband or widow or next of kin` d (p's `the reference for this person`))

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 22(2) — "for the use and benefit of the person who lacks mental capacity, until that person ceases to lack mental capacity and obtains a grant to himself or herself"
-- THE LIMITATION HAS TWO CONDITIONS AND BOTH MUST HAPPEN: ceasing to lack
-- capacity is not enough, the person must also obtain a grant. Contrast
-- s 21(2), where attaining majority is likewise not enough on its own.
`s 22(2) — the limitation on a grant made for the use and benefit of a person who lacks mental capacity` MEANS
    `limited in some other way`
        "until that person ceases to lack mental capacity and obtains a grant to himself or herself"


§§ `Sections 23 to 27 — limited grants, accrual, exception and expiry`

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 23 — "Where a person dies, leaving property in which he had no beneficial interest on his own account, and does not leave a representative who is able and willing to act, letters of administration, limited to such property, may be granted to the person beneficially interested in the property, or to some other person on his behalf."
-- "able and willing" IS ENCODED AS WILLING ONLY, and the shortfall is stated
-- rather than hidden: `Person` carries `willing to act` and carries nothing
-- for ability, so a representative who is willing but unable reads here as one
-- who is able and willing. Reported as a domain gap.
--
-- "a representative" IS NOT THE SAME THING AS A GRANTEE UNDER A GRANT THAT HAS
-- ALREADY BEEN MADE. An earlier
-- draft read the phrase over `the personal representatives of the estate`
-- alone, which is built from grants; so an executor appointed by the will who
-- was ready to act but had not yet extracted probate was invisible, and the
-- s 23 route opened against a standing executor. This module says the contrary
-- in terms two sections later, in the note on s 25(1): executors take by
-- accrual in every case, because their title comes from the will and not from
-- the grant. An
-- executor whose appointment stands is a representative the deceased left, and
-- both sources are consulted below.
--
-- WHOSE APPOINTMENT STANDS is s 13(1)(b)'s question, and it is asked with
-- s 13(1)(b)'s own predicate rather than a second one: an executor who is
-- legally incapable of acting (including by infancy under s 21(1) or want of
-- capacity under s 22(1)) or who has renounced is not a representative able to
-- act. That is why this decision takes a date, which s 23 itself does not
-- mention: infancy ends.
GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`, `the date` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 23 — letters of administration limited to trust property may be granted` d `the date` MEANS
        "Where a person dies, leaving property in which he had no beneficial interest on his own account,"
    ... (    d's `the estate`'s `the value of any property which the deceased possessed or was entitled to as trustee and not beneficially, situated in Singapore`
        PLUS d's `the estate`'s `the value of any property which the deceased possessed or was entitled to as trustee and not beneficially, situated outside Singapore`)
          GREATER THAN 0
    ... "and does not leave a representative who is able and willing to act,"
    AND NOT (`s 23 — the deceased leaves a representative who is able and willing to act` d `the date`)

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 23 — "does not leave a representative who is able and willing to act"
GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`, `the date` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 23 — the deceased leaves a representative who is able and willing to act` d `the date` MEANS
        any (GIVEN gr YIELD `a representative willing to act` gr)
            (`the personal representatives of the estate` d)
    OR  any (GIVEN x YIELD `an executor whose appointment stands and who is willing to act` x `the date`)
            (`the executors appointed by the will of the deceased` d)

-- AND HE MUST BE ALIVE, WHICH s 13(1)(b) DOES NOT ASK. s 13(1)'s five limbs
-- divide the ground between them: (b) is disqualification, and DEATH is limbs
-- (c) and (d). So `s 13(1)(b) — this executor is disqualified` is blind to
-- death by design, and borrowing it here without more let a DEAD executor
-- count as "a representative who is able and willing to act" and shut the s 23
-- route. In the limiting case it reached an executor who PREDECEASED the
-- testator — a person who never became a representative at all, and whom this
-- module's own `s 13(1)(c) — no executor survives the testator` exists to
-- identify. `has died` is conjoined for that reason, and the predeceasing
-- executor is asserted below because it is the case that cannot be defended on
-- any reading.
GIVEN x IS AN `Executor appointed by a will`, `the date` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`an executor whose appointment stands and who is willing to act` x `the date` MEANS
        NOT (`has died` (x's `the executor`))
    AND NOT (`s 13(1)(b) — this executor is disqualified` x `the date`)
    AND x's `the executor`'s `willing to act`

GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A LIST OF `Executor appointed by a will`
`the executors appointed by the will of the deceased` d MEANS
    CONSIDER d's `the will`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN EMPTY
    WHEN JUST w  THEN w's `the executors appointed by the will`

-- A CORPORATE REPRESENTATIVE IS DEEMED WILLING, and the default is stated
-- rather than left to be found: `willing to act` lives on `Person`, and a trust
-- corporation holding a grant is a `Person or corporation` with no such field.
-- The default runs AGAINST the s 23 route — an estate whose only personal
-- representative is a trust company can never be one that "does not leave a
-- representative who is able and willing to act" — which is the conservative
-- direction, s 23 displacing a representative who is already in office. The
-- ontology would need `willing to act` on `Person or corporation` to state it
-- otherwise.
--
-- A DEAD GRANTEE IS NOT A REPRESENTATIVE THE DECEASED "LEAVES", and the death
-- check is made here, per grantee, rather than by filtering the list this
-- decision reads — `the personal representatives of the estate` is shared with
-- s 55(1)(f), which NEEDS the dead ones. The corporate default is untouched:
-- `this grantee has died` is FALSE of a trust corporation for the reason given
-- at s 25(1), and `the individual in` is NOTHING of one, so this arm is not
-- reached for a corporation at all.
GIVEN gr IS A Grantee
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a representative willing to act` gr MEANS
    CONSIDER `the individual in` (gr's `the grantee`)
    WHEN JUST p  THEN NOT (`has died` p) AND p's `willing to act`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN TRUE

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 23 — "letters of administration, limited to such property"
-- Mapped to the Wills Act s 28(4)(a) category by `family-domain.l4`, which
-- reads s 23's property as "trust property" for that Act's purposes.
`s 23 — the limitation on a grant of trust property` MEANS
    `limited to settled land or to trust property`

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 24 — "In any case in which it appears necessary for preserving the property of a deceased person, the court may grant to any person whom the court thinks fit letters of administration limited to the collection and preservation of the property of the deceased, and giving discharges of debts due to his estate, subject to the directions of the court."
-- THE GATE IS EVALUATIVE ("In any case in which it appears necessary") and the
-- grantee is at large ("any person whom the court thinks fit"), so neither is
-- encoded. The LIMITATION is fixed and is encoded.
`s 24 — the limitation on a grant to collect and preserve property` MEANS
    `a grant that does not permit the distribution of the testator’s estate`

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 25(1) — "Where probate or letters of administration have been granted to more than one executor or administrator, and one of them dies, the representation of the estate shall accrue to the surviving executor or executors or, except in cases to which section 6 applies, administrator or administrators."
-- AMBIGUITY A9 — "except in cases to which section 6 applies".
--   Reading (i): the s 6(2) cases — a minority or a life interest — where the
--     Act requires either a trust corporation or two individuals, so accrual
--     to a single surviving administrator is exactly the outcome s 6(2)
--     forbids and the exception exists to stop it.
--   Reading (ii): any case governed by s 6, which is every grant, so
--     administrators never take by accrual at all.
-- TAKEN: reading (i). Reading (ii) makes the words "administrator or
-- administrators" in the same sentence inoperative, and an exception that
-- swallows the class it qualifies is not a reading a court would reach.
--
-- NOTE THE ASYMMETRY THE SUBSECTION DRAWS: the exception is attached to
-- administrators only. Executors take by accrual in every case, because their
-- title comes from the will and not from the grant.
DECLARE `To whom the representation of the estate accrues on the death of one of several personal representatives` IS ONE OF
    `section 25(1) does not apply`
    `the representation of the estate accrues to the surviving executor or executors`
    `the representation of the estate accrues to the surviving administrator or administrators`
    `the representation does not accrue, this being a case to which section 6 applies`

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 25(1)
GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`, g IS A Grant, `the date` IS A DATE
GIVETH A `To whom the representation of the estate accrues on the death of one of several personal representatives`
`s 25(1) — death of one of several executors or administrators` d g `the date` MEANS
    BRANCH IF NOT (   count (g's `the grantees`) GREATER THAN 1
                  AND any (GIVEN gr YIELD `this grantee has died` gr) (g's `the grantees`))
              THEN `section 25(1) does not apply`
           IF g's `the kind of grant` EQUALS `probate`
              THEN `the representation of the estate accrues to the surviving executor or executors`
           IF `s 6(2) — the requirement as to the grantees is engaged` d `the date`
              THEN `the representation does not accrue, this being a case to which section 6 applies`
           OTHERWISE `the representation of the estate accrues to the surviving administrator or administrators`

-- A CORPORATE GRANTEE IS DEEMED NEVER TO HAVE DIED, and the default is stated
-- rather than left to be found. `Vital status` lives on `Person`, and the Act
-- speaks of death — s 25(1) "one of them dies", s 55(1)(f) "the death of a last
-- surviving executor or administrator" — which a corporation does not do; it is
-- wound up, and nothing in the shared ontology records that. The default runs
-- AGAINST both provisions firing: s 25(1) never accrues away from a trust
-- corporation, and s 55(1)(f) can never hold of an estate whose personal
-- representative is one, however long the Public Trustee waits. That is the
-- conservative direction for two rules that move representation away from a
-- representative in office, but it is a default and not the Act's own words.
GIVEN gr IS A Grantee
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`this grantee has died` gr MEANS
    CONSIDER `the individual in` (gr's `the grantee`)
    WHEN JUST p  THEN `has died` p
    WHEN NOTHING THEN FALSE

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 25(2) — "On the death of an administrator, letters of administration may be granted in respect of any estate not fully administered and in granting such letters of administration the court shall be guided by the same rules as apply to original grants."
GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`, g IS A Grant
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 25(2) — letters of administration may be granted in respect of any estate not fully administered` d g MEANS
        "On the death of an administrator,"
    ... any (GIVEN gr YIELD    gr's `the capacity in which the grant is made` EQUALS `as administrator`
                           AND `this grantee has died` gr)
            (g's `the grantees`)
    ... "letters of administration may be granted in respect of any estate not fully administered"
    AND NOT (d's `the estate`'s `fully administered`)

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 26(1) — "A grant of probate or letters of administration may be made subject to such exception as the will or the circumstances of the case require."; s 26(2) — "A further grant may be made of the part of the estate excepted under subsection (1)."
-- s 26(1)'s antecedent is at large ("as the will or the circumstances of the
-- case require") and is not encoded. s 26(2) IS determinate: a further grant is
-- available exactly where a part was excepted, and the ontology records which
-- part.
GIVEN g IS A Grant
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 26(2) — a further grant may be made of the part of the estate excepted` g MEANS
    isJust (g's `the exception subject to which the grant is made`)

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 27 — "When a limited grant has expired by effluxion of time or the happening of the event or contingency on which it was limited, and there is still some part of the deceased’s estate unadministered, letters of administration shall be granted to those persons to whom original grants might have been made."
-- MANDATORY — "letters of administration shall be granted" — which is why it
-- also appears below among the Act's deontic outcomes. The gate has three
-- conjuncts and all three are in the ontology: the grant was a limited one, it
-- has expired, and the estate is not fully administered.
GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`, g IS A Grant
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 27 — letters of administration shall be granted to those persons to whom original grants might have been made` d g MEANS
        "When a limited grant"
    ... NOT (g's `the limitation` EQUALS `not limited`)
    ... "has expired by effluxion of time or the happening of the event or contingency on which it was limited,"
    ... g's `expired by effluxion of time or by the happening of the event or contingency on which it was limited`
    ... "and there is still some part of the deceased’s estate unadministered,"
    AND NOT (d's `the estate`'s `fully administered`)


§ `Part 5 — Security`

-- Part 4 (the oath, s 28) is NOT encoded: s 28(1) imposes a duty in the
-- prescribed form and s 28(2) exempts the Public Trustee and a trust company,
-- and neither produces anything the grant machinery reads. s 28 does appear
-- below among the Act's deontic outcomes, where the duty and its exemption are
-- the whole content.

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 29(2), s 29(3), s 29(4), s 29(5), s 29(7), s 29(8)
-- THE OUTCOME IS A TOTAL ENUM AND THE SOURCE NAMES THE ABSENT CASE TWICE:
-- s 29(4) — "he shall not be required to give security" — and s 29(8) — "shall
-- not be required to give security for the due administration of the estate".
-- A MAYBE would fold the Act's own named outcome into a bookkeeping absence.
--
-- THE AMOUNT RIDES ON THE TWO MEMBERS THAT HAVE ONE, because s 29(2) fixes it
-- ("in the amount at which the estate within the jurisdiction is sworn") and
-- s 29(5) removes only the SURETIES, not the bond.
DECLARE `The security required on a grant` IS ONE OF
    `no security is required`
    `security by bond in the prescribed form by the grantee and 2 sureties` HAS `the amount of the bond` IS A Money
    `security by bond in the prescribed form by the grantee, sureties being dispensed with` HAS `the amount of the bond` IS A Money

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 29(2) — "The security shall ordinarily be by bond in the prescribed form by the grantee and 2 sureties in the amount at which the estate within the jurisdiction is sworn, without deduction of any debts due by the deceased, other than debts secured by mortgage."
-- "the estate within the jurisdiction" is the SINGAPORE property, not the
-- whole estate. "without deduction of any debts due by the deceased, other
-- than debts secured by mortgage" is a double negative that resolves to: take
-- the gross Singapore value and deduct the mortgage-secured debts alone.
--
-- DOMAIN GAP, and it is an over-deduction. `Creditor` carries `secured`, which
-- is wider than "secured by mortgage" — s 29(2) names mortgages and the First
-- Schedule's bankruptcy rules speak of secured creditors generally. An estate
-- with a debt secured otherwise than by mortgage (a pledge, a charge over
-- book debts) will compute a bond smaller than s 29(2) requires. The narrower
-- fact is not in the ontology and is not invented here.
GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A Money
`s 29(2) — the amount at which the estate within the jurisdiction is sworn` d MEANS
         `the value of the property in Singapore` (d's `the estate`)
    MINUS sum (map (GIVEN c YIELD c's `the debt`)
                   (filter (GIVEN c YIELD c's `secured`)
                           (d's `the estate`'s `the creditors of the deceased`)))

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 29(4) — "Where the Public Trustee has obtained a grant of letters of administration, he shall not be required to give security."
-- PER GRANTEE, BECAUSE THE SUBSECTION EXEMPTS A PERSON AND NOT A GRANT: "he
-- shall not be required to give security" — "he" is the Public Trustee. So a
-- grant to the Public Trustee and an individual jointly exempts the first and
-- leaves the second bound. An earlier draft tested this with `all` over the
-- grantees, which made one individual co-grantee re-impose security on the
-- Public Trustee himself — the opposite of what s 29(4) says. The same
-- correction applies to s 29(8), whose subject is likewise "A grantee".
GIVEN gr IS A Grantee
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 29(4) — this grantee is the Public Trustee` gr MEANS
    (gr's `the grantee`) EQUALS `the Public Trustee`

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 29(8) — "A grantee of letters of administration from a Family Court shall not be required to give security for the due administration of the estate unless — (a) the person for whose use and benefit the grant is made is an infant; or (b) the Family Court thinks fit to require such security."
-- "shall not ... unless", and encoded THAT WAY ROUND: the exemption is the
-- rule and the two limbs are exceptions to it. Written the other way round —
-- as a requirement conditioned on the two limbs — the burden would sit on the
-- wrong party.
--
-- LIMB (a) IS A JOIN — GUARDIANSHIP OF INFANTS ACT 1934. "the person for whose
-- use and benefit the grant is made" is `Grantee`'s reference field, which is
-- JUST only for the three representative capacities; infancy is then the same
-- majority-date question as s 6(2) and s 21.
--
-- THE SUBJECT IS "A grantee of letters of administration", and the kind of
-- grant is tested for that reason. s 29(8) exempts a grantee of letters of
-- administration and says nothing about a grantee of probate, so no s 29(8)
-- exemption is made out on a Family Court grant of probate. An earlier draft
-- tested only that the grant came from a Family Court, so this decision
-- reported an exemption for a class the subsection does not mention. The
-- s 29(7) BRANCH below happened to hide it — it disposes of probate before
-- reaching here — but a decision has to be true of its own facts and not only
-- of the path its one caller takes.
--
-- AND WHICH KIND OF LETTERS. This is A5's and A11's fork asked a third time,
-- and it is NOT registered as an ambiguity because the Act resolves it in
-- terms rather than leaving it open. s 29(7) is the duty this subsection
-- excuses, and it opens "Subject to subsection (8), in the case of
-- administrations whether with or without the will annexed". A subsection
-- expressly made subject to this one, over a class expressly stated as both
-- kinds, cannot be carved out of by an exception that reaches only one of them
-- without leaving "Subject to subsection (8)" partly inoperative. So BOTH
-- kinds, on the Act's own words and not on the balance of reasons A5 and A11
-- had to weigh. Recorded here because the direction runs the unconservative
-- way — a wide reading EXPANDS an exemption from security, which is the
-- opposite of the direction A11 could rely on — so a reader is owed the
-- textual ground rather than a silent choice.
GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`, gr IS A Grantee, `the date` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 29(8)(a) — the person for whose use and benefit the grant is made is an infant` d gr `the date` MEANS
    CONSIDER gr's `the reference for the person for whose use and benefit the grant is made`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN FALSE
    WHEN JUST r  THEN any (GIVEN i YIELD    (i's `the infant`'s `the reference for this person`) EQUALS r
                                        AND ((i's `the infant`) `is an infant on` `the date`))
                          (d's `the infants concerned in the estate`)

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 29(8)
GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`
      g IS A Grant
      gr IS A Grantee
      f IS A `The further facts on which the grant machinery turns`
      `the date` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 29(8) — this grantee of letters of administration from a Family Court shall not be required to give security` d g gr f `the date` MEANS
        "A grantee of letters of administration from a Family Court"
    ... (   g's `the kind of grant` EQUALS `letters of administration`
         OR g's `the kind of grant` EQUALS `letters of administration with the will annexed`)
    ... `the grant was made by a Family Court` g
    ... "shall not be required to give security for the due administration of the estate unless —"
    AND NOT (   "(a) the person for whose use and benefit the grant is made is an infant; or"
             .. `s 29(8)(a) — the person for whose use and benefit the grant is made is an infant` d gr `the date`
             .. "(b) the Family Court thinks fit to require such security."
             OR f's `the Family Court thinks fit to require such security`)

GIVEN g IS A Grant
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the grant was made by a Family Court` g MEANS
    CONSIDER g's `the court that made the grant`
    WHEN `a court within the meaning of these Acts` c THEN c EQUALS `a Family Court`
    OTHERWISE FALSE

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 29(7) — "Subject to subsection (8), in the case of administrations whether with or without the will annexed the person to whom the grant is made or on whose behalf it is sealed shall give security for the due administration of the estate."; s 29(2), s 29(4), s 29(5), s 29(8)
-- THE WHOLE OF s 29's REQUIREMENT, ASSEMBLED IN THE ORDER THE SECTION IMPOSES
-- IT. s 29(7) is the duty and it is expressly "Subject to subsection (8)";
-- s 29(4) is an independent exemption for the Public Trustee that s 29(7) is
-- not made subject to but that plainly prevails, being a "shall not be
-- required"; s 29(2) then fixes the ordinary form and amount and s 29(5)
-- ordinarily dispenses with the sureties in one case.
--
-- s 29(1), (3) and (6) ARE NOT ENCODED and each is a discretion or an
-- evidential power: (1) "the registrar shall determine its sufficiency";
-- (3) "may for any sufficient reason increase or decrease the number of the
-- sureties, or dispense with them, or reduce the amount of the bond";
-- (6) "Sureties may be required by the registrar to justify". This decision
-- therefore states the ORDINARY position, which is what s 29(2) and s 29(5)
-- both say in terms ("shall ordinarily be", "may ordinarily be dispensed
-- with"), and any of ss 29(1), (3) and (6) may displace it.
GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`
      g IS A Grant
      gr IS A Grantee
      f IS A `The further facts on which the grant machinery turns`
      `the date` IS A DATE
GIVETH A `The security required on a grant`
`s 29 — the security ordinarily required of this grantee` d g gr f `the date` MEANS
    BRANCH IF NOT (   g's `the kind of grant` EQUALS `letters of administration`
                   OR g's `the kind of grant` EQUALS `letters of administration with the will annexed`)
              THEN `no security is required`
           IF `s 29(4) — this grantee is the Public Trustee` gr
              THEN `no security is required`
           IF `s 29(8) — this grantee of letters of administration from a Family Court shall not be required to give security` d g gr f `the date`
              THEN `no security is required`
           IF f's `the administrator is entitled to the whole of the estate after payment of the debts`
              THEN `security by bond in the prescribed form by the grantee, sureties being dispensed with`
                       (`s 29(2) — the amount at which the estate within the jurisdiction is sworn` d)
           OTHERWISE `security by bond in the prescribed form by the grantee and 2 sureties`
                       (`s 29(2) — the amount at which the estate within the jurisdiction is sworn` d)

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 29(7), s 55(1)(c)
-- THE GRANT-LEVEL VIEW, DERIVED AND NOT PRIMARY. s 29 binds grantees one by
-- one, so the only honest whole-grant question is whether security is required
-- of ANY of them. s 55(1)(c) needs exactly that question and no finer one,
-- because `Grant`'s `has given such security as is lawfully required to be
-- furnished` is recorded per GRANT and not per grantee — a limit of the shared
-- ontology, reported as a domain gap rather than papered over by pretending
-- the duty is the grant's.
GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`
      g IS A Grant
      f IS A `The further facts on which the grant machinery turns`
      `the date` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`security is lawfully required of some grantee` d g f `the date` MEANS
    any (GIVEN gr YIELD `security is lawfully required of this grantee` d g gr f `the date`)
        (g's `the grantees`)

GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`
      g IS A Grant
      gr IS A Grantee
      f IS A `The further facts on which the grant machinery turns`
      `the date` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`security is lawfully required of this grantee` d g gr f `the date` MEANS
    CONSIDER `s 29 — the security ordinarily required of this grantee` d g gr f `the date`
    WHEN `no security is required` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 30 — "When letters of administration are granted to a creditor, he may be required to enter into a bond to pay the debts of the deceased rateably, without preferring his own debt."
-- A SECOND, DIFFERENT BOND, and it is worth keeping separate from s 29's: this
-- one secures rateable payment, not due administration, and it bites on a
-- creditor-administrator alone. "he may be required" leaves the requiring at
-- large, so only the class is encoded.
--
-- AMBIGUITY A11 — which grants "letters of administration" reaches in s 30.
-- This is the SAME fork as AMBIGUITY A5 and it has to be answered again,
-- because A5's reasoning is internal to s 6(2) and does not carry.
--   Reading (i): both kinds, s 30 using "letters of administration" as the
--     genus, the way s 18(4)(c) does when it opens a grant "to a creditor of
--     the deceased".
--   Reading (ii): letters of administration simpliciter only, s 2 defining the
--     two as separate terms and s 29(7) ("administrations whether with or
--     without the will annexed"), s 21(2) and s 55(1)(c) all showing that the
--     Act says both in as many words when it means both.
-- TAKEN: reading (i). s 13(2)(e) expressly ranks "a creditor of the deceased"
-- for a grant of letters of administration WITH the will annexed, so reading
-- (ii) would put the very creditor s 13(2)(e) contemplates outside the one
-- section that exists to stop a creditor-administrator "preferring his own
-- debt" — and the mischief is identical either way, debts being paid before
-- the will's dispositions. s 30 also confers no burden of its own — "he may be
-- required", and the requiring is left at large — so the wide reading adds an
-- available protection rather than an obligation. An earlier draft took reading (ii) silently.
GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`, g IS A Grant
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 30 — letters of administration are granted to a creditor` d g MEANS
        (   g's `the kind of grant` EQUALS `letters of administration`
         OR g's `the kind of grant` EQUALS `letters of administration with the will annexed`)
    AND any (GIVEN gr YIELD `s 13(2)(e) — a creditor of the deceased` d (`the reference of the grantee` gr))
            (g's `the grantees`)


§ `Part 8 — District Courts`

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 34 — "34. [Repealed by Act 27 of 2014]"; s 36 — "36. [Repealed by Act 27 of 2014]"
-- PART 8 HAS THREE SECTIONS AND ONLY ONE OF THEM HAS CONTENT. ss 34 and 36 are
-- named here so that an in-order reading of this module has no silent gap: a
-- reader who finds s 33 and then s 35 cannot otherwise tell whether s 34 was
-- overlooked or is not there to be encoded. They carry no operative words in
-- the version in force.
`ss 34 and 36 — repealed, and therefore not encoded` MEANS
        "34. [Repealed by Act 27 of 2014]"
    ... "36. [Repealed by Act 27 of 2014]"

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 35(1) — "No probate or letters of administration granted by a District Court before 1 January 2015 shall be invalid by reason of the assets and effects of the deceased being subsequently found to be of a value which exceeds $3 million."
-- A VALIDATING PROVISION, and encoded as one: it says a grant is NOT invalid,
-- so the decision is TRUE where the saving applies. `family-domain.l4` widened
-- the grantor slot to three values precisely so that this case has a truthful
-- record; the value tested is the whole estate, since s 35(1) speaks of "the
-- assets and effects of the deceased" without a situs qualifier.
--
-- NAMED FOR THE SAVING, NOT FOR THE VALIDITY. This decision is TRUE exactly
-- where s 35(1) has work to do, which requires the value to exceed $3 million:
-- below the threshold the grant was within the District Court's jurisdiction
-- and needs no saving. So FALSE here means "s 35(1) does not reach this grant",
-- NEVER "the grant is invalid" — a $120,000 estate answers FALSE and the grant
-- is perfectly good. An earlier draft named this decision for the validity of
-- the grant rather than for the saving, which invited exactly the opposite
-- reading of its own FALSE.
--
-- s 35(2) and s 35(3) are NOT encoded: (2) imposes estate-duty and court-fee
-- liabilities and a security requirement measured by another court's practice,
-- and (3) makes revocation available "unless ... the grantee complies without
-- delay", which has no determinate output.
GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`, g IS A Grant
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 35(1) — the saving in section 35(1) applies to this grant` d g MEANS
        "No probate or letters of administration granted by a District Court before 1 January 2015"
    ... (g's `the court that made the grant`) EQUALS `a District Court, the grant having been made before 1 January 2015`
    ... "shall be invalid by reason of the assets and effects of the deceased being subsequently found to be of a value which exceeds $3 million."
    AND `the value of the whole estate` (d's `the estate`) GREATER THAN 3_000_000

GIVEN e IS AN Estate
GIVETH A Money
`the value of the whole estate` e MEANS
         e's `the value of the movable property situated in Singapore`
    PLUS e's `the value of the immovable property situated in Singapore`
    PLUS e's `the value of the movable property situated outside Singapore`
    PLUS e's `the value of the immovable property situated outside Singapore`


§ `Part 11 — General`

§§ `Section 55 — Grant to the Public Trustee in cases of delay`

-- SIX LIMBS, DISJUNCTIVE, and every one of them has a determinate antecedent —
-- which is what makes s 55 worth encoding where s 32 ("for any sufficient
-- cause") is not. The consequent is a permission with an alternative at large
-- ("to the Public Trustee, or to such other person as the court thinks fit"),
-- so the encoding stops at the gate and names the Public Trustee limb only.

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 55(1)(a) — "where, after the expiration of 6 months from the death of a deceased person, no application has been made for probate or letters of administration to his estate"
-- SIX MONTHS FROM THE DEATH, computed with `add months` so that the
-- anniversary is a calendar one: 30 days would be wrong in every month but
-- one, and the daydate note on that function explains why an anniversary must
-- not be built by reconstructing components.
--
-- FALSE where the date of death is not known: the window cannot be opened
-- without a start, and this is the same discipline as AMBIGUITY A1.
GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`, `the date` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 55(1)(a) — six months have expired from the death and no application has been made` d `the date` MEANS
        `six months have expired from the death` d `the date`
    AND null (d's `the probate applications made in respect of the estate`)

GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`, `the date` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`six months have expired from the death` d `the date` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the date of death of` (d's `the deceased`)
    WHEN NOTHING THEN FALSE
    WHEN JUST dd THEN (`add months` dd 6) `is before` `the date`

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 55(1)(b) — "where any such application, though made within the said 6 months, has not within that period been proceeded with, or has been withdrawn or refused"
-- THREE FATES, NOT ONE, and the ontology names them: `not proceeded with`,
-- `withdrawn`, `refused`. `still pending` and `proceeded with` are the two
-- that do NOT satisfy this limb, which is why the enum has five members.
GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 55(1)(b) — an application made within the 6 months has not been proceeded with, or has been withdrawn or refused` d MEANS
    any (GIVEN a YIELD `an application within the six months that came to nothing` d a)
        (d's `the probate applications made in respect of the estate`)

GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`, a IS A `Probate application`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`an application within the six months that came to nothing` d a MEANS
        `the application was made within 6 months of the death` d a
    AND (   "has not within that period been proceeded with,"
         .. a's `what became of the application` EQUALS `not proceeded with`
         .. "or has been withdrawn"
         .. a's `what became of the application` EQUALS `withdrawn`
         .. "or refused"
         OR a's `what became of the application` EQUALS `refused`)

GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`, a IS A `Probate application`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the application was made within 6 months of the death` d a MEANS
    CONSIDER a's `the date of the application`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN FALSE
    WHEN JUST da THEN
        CONSIDER `the date of death of` (d's `the deceased`)
        WHEN NOTHING THEN FALSE
        WHEN JUST dd THEN NOT (da `is after` (`add months` dd 6))

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 55(1)(c) — "where any person, who has received a grant of letters of administration with or without the will annexed, neglects within one month of the date of the grant to give such security as he is lawfully required to furnish"
-- ONE MONTH, CALENDAR, from the date of the grant. This limb is the LEST arm
-- of the s 29(7) duty below: the Act supplies both a deadline and a
-- consequence for missing it, which is rare enough in this Act to be worth
-- saying, and it is why s 29(7) can be written as a regulative rule at all.
-- "such security as he is lawfully required to furnish" — SO WHERE s 29
-- requires none, there is nothing to neglect and the limb cannot fire. An
-- earlier draft omitted that conjunct and tested only whether security had
-- been given, which made the limb TRUE for every Family Court grantee exempted
-- by s 29(8) — the exact class the Act had just excused. Caught by the
-- worked example below, which is why it is kept.
GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`
      g IS A Grant
      f IS A `The further facts on which the grant machinery turns`
      `the date` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 55(1)(c) — the grantee neglects within one month of the date of the grant to give such security as he is lawfully required to furnish` d g f `the date` MEANS
        "where any person, who has received a grant of letters of administration with or without the will annexed,"
    ... (   g's `the kind of grant` EQUALS `letters of administration`
         OR g's `the kind of grant` EQUALS `letters of administration with the will annexed`)
    ... "to give such security as he is lawfully required to furnish"
    ... `security is lawfully required of some grantee` d g f `the date`
    ... NOT (g's `has given such security as is lawfully required to be furnished`)
    ... "neglects within one month of the date of the grant"
    AND `one month has expired from the date of the grant` g `the date`


GIVEN g IS A Grant, `the date` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`one month has expired from the date of the grant` g `the date` MEANS
    CONSIDER g's `the date of the grant`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN FALSE
    WHEN JUST dg THEN (`add months` dg 1) `is before` `the date`

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 55(1)(d) — "where a receiver may be appointed under section 39, but it appears that such appointment would not be a sufficient protection for the estate"; s 39
-- PART 9 IS OUT OF SCOPE, but s 55(1)(d) reaches into it, so s 39's gate is
-- reproduced here to exactly the depth this limb needs and no further: a
-- person leaving property within Singapore, and a danger of waste. The rest of
-- s 39 — who may apply, whom the court may appoint — is not encoded.
GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`, f IS A `The further facts on which the grant machinery turns`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 55(1)(d) — a receiver may be appointed under section 39, but the appointment would not be a sufficient protection` d f MEANS
        "Where any person dies leaving property within Singapore"
    ... `the value of the property in Singapore` (d's `the estate`) GREATER THAN 0
    ... "that there is danger that the property may be wasted,"
    ... f's `there is danger that the property may be wasted`
    ... "but it appears that such appointment would not be a sufficient protection for the estate;"
    AND f's `it appears that such appointment would not be a sufficient protection for the estate`

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 55(1)(e) — "where an administrator has failed to extract the grant of letters of administration"
GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 55(1)(e) — an administrator has failed to extract the grant of letters of administration` d MEANS
    any (GIVEN g YIELD `a grant of letters of administration that has not been extracted` g)
        (d's `the grants made in respect of the estate`)

GIVEN g IS A Grant
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a grant of letters of administration that has not been extracted` g MEANS
        (   g's `the kind of grant` EQUALS `letters of administration`
         OR g's `the kind of grant` EQUALS `letters of administration with the will annexed`)
    AND NOT (g's `has been extracted`)

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 55(1)(f) — "where after the death of a last surviving executor or administrator of a deceased person’s estate 6 months have elapsed and no application for the representation of the estate has been made"
-- "a last surviving" — SO EVERY personal representative must have died, and
-- the six months run from the LAST of those deaths. An estate with no personal
-- representative at all has no last surviving one, so the limb is FALSE there;
-- that case is s 55(1)(a)'s, not this one's.
--
-- FALSE where any of the deaths is undated, for the AMBIGUITY A1 reason: the
-- clock cannot be started from a date that is not in evidence.
GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`, `the date` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 55(1)(f) — six months have elapsed since the death of a last surviving executor or administrator, and no application has been made` d `the date` MEANS
        NOT (null (`the personal representatives of the estate` d))
    AND all (GIVEN gr YIELD `this grantee has died` gr) (`the personal representatives of the estate` d)
    AND `six months have elapsed since the last of the representatives died` d `the date`
    AND null (d's `the probate applications made in respect of the estate`)

GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`, `the date` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`six months have elapsed since the last of the representatives died` d `the date` MEANS
    all (GIVEN gr YIELD `six months have elapsed since this grantee died` gr `the date`)
        (`the personal representatives of the estate` d)

GIVEN gr IS A Grantee, `the date` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`six months have elapsed since this grantee died` gr `the date` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the individual in` (gr's `the grantee`)
    WHEN NOTHING THEN FALSE
    WHEN JUST p  THEN
        CONSIDER `the date of death of` p
        WHEN NOTHING THEN FALSE
        WHEN JUST dp THEN (`add months` dp 6) `is before` `the date`

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 55(1) — "letters of administration with or without the will annexed may be granted to the Public Trustee, or to such other person as the court thinks fit."
GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`
      g IS A Grant
      f IS A `The further facts on which the grant machinery turns`
      `the date` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 55(1) — letters of administration may be granted to the Public Trustee in a case of delay` d g f `the date` MEANS
        "(a) where, after the expiration of 6 months from the death of a deceased person, no application has been made for probate or letters of administration to his estate;"
    ..  `s 55(1)(a) — six months have expired from the death and no application has been made` d `the date`
    ..  "(b) where any such application, though made within the said 6 months, has not within that period been proceeded with, or has been withdrawn or refused;"
    ..  `s 55(1)(b) — an application made within the 6 months has not been proceeded with, or has been withdrawn or refused` d
    ..  "(c) where any person, who has received a grant of letters of administration with or without the will annexed, neglects within one month of the date of the grant to give such security as he is lawfully required to furnish;"
    ..  `s 55(1)(c) — the grantee neglects within one month of the date of the grant to give such security as he is lawfully required to furnish` d g f `the date`
    ..  "(d) where a receiver may be appointed under section 39, but it appears that such appointment would not be a sufficient protection for the estate;"
    ..  `s 55(1)(d) — a receiver may be appointed under section 39, but the appointment would not be a sufficient protection` d f
    ..  "(e) where an administrator has failed to extract the grant of letters of administration; or"
    ..  `s 55(1)(e) — an administrator has failed to extract the grant of letters of administration` d
    ..  "(f) where after the death of a last surviving executor or administrator of a deceased person’s estate 6 months have elapsed and no application for the representation of the estate has been made,"
    OR  `s 55(1)(f) — six months have elapsed since the death of a last surviving executor or administrator, and no application has been made` d `the date`

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 55(2) — "Nothing in this section shall be construed so as to prevent the Public Trustee from applying for or being granted letters of administration of the estate of a deceased person with or without the will annexed before the expiration of a period of 6 months of the death of the deceased."
-- A SAVING, not a power: it says the six-month periods in s 55(1)(a), (b) and
-- (f) are not a bar to the Public Trustee acting sooner under some other
-- provision. It confers nothing, so it has no operative encoding and is
-- carried inert.
`s 55(2) — the six-month periods do not prevent the Public Trustee from applying sooner` MEANS
    "Nothing in this section shall be construed so as to prevent the Public Trustee from applying for or being granted letters of administration of the estate of a deceased person with or without the will annexed before the expiration of a period of 6 months of the death of the deceased."


§§ `Section 56(2) — the $500 route where no administration has been taken out`

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 56(2) — "If no administration has been taken out to the estate of such deceased person who has died intestate, and whose assets do not exceed $500, including the amount of the funds directed to be so paid, transferred or delivered to him, the funds may be paid, transferred or delivered to the person who, being a widower, widow, child, father, mother, brother or sister of the deceased, would be entitled to take administration to his or her estate upon a declaration by that person in the prescribed form."
-- s 56(1) IS NOT ENCODED: it is a rule about who funds IN COURT may be paid to
-- on the death of a payee, and its machinery (orders, certificates of the
-- registrar, the trustee/own-right distinction) is not grant machinery.
-- s 56(2) IS encoded, because it is a MONEY THRESHOLD with a NAMED CLASS —
-- the same shape as ss 62 to 64 — and because its class is narrower than
-- s 18(4)(a)'s and the difference is easy to miss: it names seven degrees
-- ("widower, widow, child, father, mother, brother or sister") and no
-- grandparent, uncle, aunt or remoter descendant.
--
-- "including the amount of the funds directed to be so paid" is why the test
-- adds the funds in court to the assets rather than comparing them separately.
GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A Money
`s 56(2) — the assets of the deceased, including the funds directed to be paid` d MEANS
         `the value of the whole estate` (d's `the estate`)
    PLUS d's `the estate`'s `the amount of the funds in court directed to be paid, transferred or delivered to him`

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 56(2) — "the person who, being a widower, widow, child, father, mother, brother or sister of the deceased, would be entitled to take administration to his or her estate"
-- SURVIVORSHIP, for the same reason as `the next of kin of the deceased`: the
-- class is defined by who "would be entitled to take administration", and a
-- relative who predeceased the intestate would not. The spouse limb carries
-- its own filter inside `the husband or widow of the deceased`; the other five
-- degrees are filtered here.
GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A LIST OF Person
`the persons named in section 56(2)` d MEANS
    concat
      (LIST `the husband or widow of the deceased` d
          , filter (GIVEN p YIELD (p `survives` (d's `the deceased`)))
                   (concat
                     (LIST map (GIVEN c YIELD c's `the child`) (d's `the deceased`'s `children`)
                         , maybeToList (d's `the family`'s `the father of the deceased`)
                         , maybeToList (d's `the family`'s `the mother of the deceased`)
                         , map (GIVEN r YIELD r's `the relative`) (d's `the family`'s `the brothers and sisters of the deceased`))))

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 56(2)
GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`, `the reference` IS A STRING
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 56(2) — the funds may be paid to this person on a declaration in the prescribed form` d `the reference` MEANS
        "If no administration has been taken out to the estate of such deceased person"
    ... null (d's `the grants made in respect of the estate`)
    ... "who has died intestate,"
    ... `s 18(1) — the person has died intestate` d
    ... "and whose assets do not exceed $500, including the amount of the funds directed to be so paid, transferred or delivered to him,"
    ... `s 56(2) — the assets of the deceased, including the funds directed to be paid` d AT MOST 500
    ... "the funds may be paid, transferred or delivered to the person who, being a widower, widow, child, father, mother, brother or sister of the deceased,"
    AND any (GIVEN p YIELD (p's `the reference for this person`) EQUALS `the reference`)
            (`the persons named in section 56(2)` d)


§§ `Section 62 — Administration by the Public Trustee`

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 62(2)(b) — "in the case of a person who dies on or after 17 September 2005"
-- A COMMENCEMENT TEST ON THE DATE OF DEATH. FALSE where the date of death is
-- not known, on the AMBIGUITY A1 discipline: an exclusion is a positive fact
-- and an unproved one is not made out. Note that this reading makes the
-- $50,000 base LARGER, so it works against the Public Trustee's power and not
-- in favour of it, which is the conservative direction for a provision that
-- displaces a court grant.
GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 62(2)(b) — the deceased died on or after 17 September 2005` d MEANS
    CONSIDER `the date of death of` (d's `the deceased`)
    WHEN NOTHING THEN FALSE
    WHEN JUST dd THEN NOT (dd `is before` (YMD 2005 9 17))

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 62(1) — "property in Singapore not exceeding $50,000 in value (without deduction for debts)"; s 62(2)(a)-(b)
-- "without deduction for debts" is an instruction NOT to subtract, so the
-- debts do not appear here at all. What IS subtracted is only what s 62(2)
-- says: the trustee property, and — for a death on or after 17 September 2005
-- — the Dependants' Protection Insurance moneys.
--
-- THE TRUSTEE EXCLUSION IS TAKEN AT ITS SINGAPORE FIGURE ONLY, because the
-- base is the Singapore property. `family-domain.l4` splits that exclusion by
-- situs for exactly this reason: subtracting an undivided trustee figure from
-- a Singapore-only base would over-subtract whenever any of the trustee
-- property sits abroad.
GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A Money
`s 62 — the value of the property in Singapore for the purposes of section 62` d MEANS
          `the value of the property in Singapore` (d's `the estate`)
    MINUS d's `the estate`'s `the value of any property which the deceased possessed or was entitled to as trustee and not beneficially, situated in Singapore`
    MINUS (IF   `s 62(2)(b) — the deceased died on or after 17 September 2005` d
           THEN d's `the estate`'s `the value of any moneys payable by an appointed insurer pursuant to the Dependants’ Protection Insurance Scheme or any other equivalent scheme maintained by the Central Provident Fund Board`
           ELSE 0)

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 62(1) — "the Public Trustee, after satisfying himself that no application for letters of administration is pending, may, if he thinks fit, by writing signed by him declare that he undertakes to administer such property"
-- TWO GATES AND A DISCRETION. The gates — the $50,000 threshold and no pending
-- application — are encoded; "if he thinks fit" is not, and the declaration
-- itself is a fact recorded on `The further facts on which the grant machinery
-- turns`, not an output of this rule.
GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 62(1) — the Public Trustee may declare that he undertakes to administer the property` d MEANS
        "Where any person dies leaving property in Singapore not exceeding $50,000 in value (without deduction for debts),"
    ... `s 62 — the value of the property in Singapore for the purposes of section 62` d AT MOST 50_000
    ... "after satisfying himself that no application for letters of administration is pending,"
    AND NOT (any (GIVEN a YIELD `a pending application for letters of administration` a)
                 (d's `the probate applications made in respect of the estate`))

GIVEN a IS A `Probate application`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a pending application for letters of administration` a MEANS
        (   a's `the grant applied for` EQUALS `letters of administration`
         OR a's `the grant applied for` EQUALS `letters of administration with the will annexed`)
    AND a's `what became of the application` EQUALS `still pending`

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 62(3) — "Upon a declaration under subsection (1), the Public Trustee shall be empowered to administer such property as though letters of administration, with or without the will annexed, of the estate of the deceased person had been granted to him"
-- A DEEMING PROVISION, and its only antecedent is that the declaration was
-- made — s 62(3) does not re-test the s 62(1) gates, which is why this rule
-- does not either. s 62(4) (notice to the registrar) and s 62(5) (the Public
-- Trustee declining to proceed "if he thinks it expedient") are not encoded.
GIVEN f IS A `The further facts on which the grant machinery turns`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 62(3) — the Public Trustee is empowered to administer the property as though letters of administration had been granted to him` f MEANS
    f's `the Public Trustee has by writing signed by him declared that he undertakes to administer such property`


§§ `Section 63 — Payment for a minor’s maintenance out of property held by the Public Trustee`

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 63(1)-(2)
-- A BANDED THRESHOLD, and the two bands are exactly complementary at $25,000:
-- s 63(1) is "not exceeding $25,000" and s 63(2) is "exceeds $25,000 but does
-- not exceed $50,000". Above $50,000 the section confers NO power, which is
-- why the absent member is named for section 63 rather than for payment at
-- large — other powers may reach such a fund; this section does not.
DECLARE `The payments the Public Trustee may make for a minor’s maintenance under section 63` IS ONE OF
    `no payment may be made under section 63`
    `payments of the whole or such part of the income and capital money of such property as may in all the circumstances be reasonable`
    `payments of capital money to the extent of $25,000`

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 63(1) — "during the minority of any such person"
-- JOIN — GUARDIANSHIP OF INFANTS ACT 1934, the fourth and last of the four
-- provisions of this Act that ask the infancy question (s 6(2), s 21,
-- s 29(8)(a), s 63(1)). "any such person" is the person for whom the
-- Public Trustee holds the property on trust; the ontology's nearest record of
-- such a person is an infant concerned in the estate.
GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`, `the date` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 63(1) — the property is held upon trust for a person during whose minority payments may be made` d `the date` MEANS
    any (GIVEN i YIELD ((i's `the infant`) `is an infant on` `the date`))
        (d's `the infants concerned in the estate`)

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 63(1)-(2)
-- "subject to any prior interest or charges affecting that property" is NOT
-- computed, and the reason is that it qualifies the AMOUNT of a payment whose
-- amount is already at the Public Trustee's "sole discretion" and measured by
-- what "may in all the circumstances be reasonable". There is no determinate
-- quantity for the qualification to reduce. It rides inert.
--
-- s 63(3) — to whom the payments may be made, and the two things that do not
-- matter — is NOT encoded: "or otherwise as the Public Trustee may, in his
-- discretion, determine" is the operative half and it is a discretion.
GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`, `the date` IS A DATE
GIVETH A `The payments the Public Trustee may make for a minor’s maintenance under section 63`
`s 63 — the payments the Public Trustee may make` d `the date` MEANS
    BRANCH IF NOT (`s 63(1) — the property is held upon trust for a person during whose minority payments may be made` d `the date`)
              THEN `no payment may be made under section 63`
           IF d's `the estate`'s `the value of the property administered by the Public Trustee` AT MOST 25_000
              THEN `payments of the whole or such part of the income and capital money of such property as may in all the circumstances be reasonable`
           IF d's `the estate`'s `the value of the property administered by the Public Trustee` AT MOST 50_000
              THEN `payments of capital money to the extent of $25,000`
           OTHERWISE `no payment may be made under section 63`


§§ `Section 64(1) — the $10,000 exemption from notice of distribution`

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 64(1) — "Where the total value of any property (without deduction for debts, but not including the value of any property which the deceased possessed or was entitled to as trustee and not beneficially) administered by the Public Trustee ... does not exceed $10,000, it shall not be necessary for the Public Trustee to give notice by advertisement in the Gazette or otherwise of his intention to distribute the estate"
-- DOMAIN GAP. The base is "any property ... administered by the Public
-- Trustee", and `Estate` records that as ONE figure with no split between the
-- beneficial and the trustee parts of it. The trustee exclusion is therefore
-- taken at the whole-estate trustee figure, which OVER-SUBTRACTS wherever the
-- Public Trustee administers only part of the estate — the s 62(1) case
-- exactly. Reported rather than hidden; the ontology would need the
-- PT-administered figure split by beneficial/trustee to state s 64(1)
-- exactly.
--
-- AND THE ERROR RUNS THE UNSAFE WAY, which is the half a reader needs and the
-- half an unquantified "over-subtracts" does not give. Over-subtracting makes
-- the base SMALLER, so the $10,000 exemption from advertising becomes MORE
-- available, and s 64(2) then protects the Public Trustee against claimants he
-- never advertised for. The computed base can even go negative, where the
-- trustee property outside the Public Trustee's hands exceeds what is in them;
-- a negative base is at most $10,000, so the exemption applies. Contrast
-- s 62(2)(b) above, where the reading taken makes the base LARGER and so works
-- against the Public Trustee's power — the conservative direction. This one
-- does not, and nothing in the ontology lets it be corrected here.
--
-- s 64(2) (the Public Trustee's protection) and s 64(3) (the two savings) are
-- NOT encoded: they are consequences of a distribution rather than tests.
GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A Money
`s 64(1) — the total value of the property administered by the Public Trustee` d MEANS
          d's `the estate`'s `the value of the property administered by the Public Trustee`
    MINUS d's `the estate`'s `the value of any property which the deceased possessed or was entitled to as trustee and not beneficially, situated in Singapore`
    MINUS d's `the estate`'s `the value of any property which the deceased possessed or was entitled to as trustee and not beneficially, situated outside Singapore`

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 64(1)
GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 64(1) — it shall not be necessary for the Public Trustee to give notice of his intention to distribute the estate` d MEANS
    `s 64(1) — the total value of the property administered by the Public Trustee` d AT MOST 10_000


§ `The Act’s own “shall” and “may” — the deontic outcomes`

-- FOUR PROVISIONS OF THIS ACT IMPOSE A DUTY OR CONFER A PERMISSION ON A NAMED
-- PARTY, and the difference between "shall" and "may" is the only thing some
-- of them decide. A constitutive predicate cannot carry that difference: it is
-- TRUE or FALSE either way. So these four are stated as regulative rules,
-- where MUST and MAY have different consequences for an omission.
--
-- ONLY ONE OF THEM HAS A DEADLINE. s 55(1)(c) gives the s 29(7) duty one
-- month from the date of the grant, and supplies the consequence of missing
-- it — letters of administration may then go to the Public Trustee. The other
-- three duties are undated in the Act, so they carry no WITHIN; writing one in
-- would be inventing a period Parliament did not enact.
--
-- THE TRACE CLOCK COUNTS TICKS, NOT CALENDAR MONTHS. `WITHIN 30` below is the
-- s 55(1)(c) month expressed for `#TRACE`, and it is an approximation made
-- HERE and nowhere else: the constitutive test,
-- `one month has expired from the date of the grant`, uses `add months` and is
-- calendar-exact.

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 12(2), s 27, s 28(1), s 29(7), s 55(1)
DECLARE `A party under the Probate and Administration Act` IS ONE OF
    `the court`
    `the registrar`
    `the grantee`

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 12(2), s 27, s 28(1), s 29(7), s 55(1)
DECLARE `An act under the Probate and Administration Act` IS ONE OF
    `take an oath in the prescribed form, faithfully to administer the estate and to account for the same`
    `give security for the due administration of the estate`
    `revoke the probate and grant a new probate of the will and codicil together`
    `grant letters of administration to those persons to whom original grants might have been made`
    `grant letters of administration with or without the will annexed to the Public Trustee`

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 28(1) — "Upon the grant of any probate or letters of administration, the grantee shall take an oath in the prescribed form, faithfully to administer the estate and to account for the same."; s 28(2) — "Subsection (1) shall not apply where the grantee is the Public Trustee or a trust company."
-- s 28(2) IS AN EXEMPTION FROM THE DUTY, not a permission to omit it, so it is
-- encoded by the duty not arising at all rather than by a MAY.
--
-- THE EXEMPT CLASS IS STATED IN s 28(2)'s OWN WORDS AND NOT BORROWED FROM
-- s 6(6). s 6(6) opens "For the purposes of this section", so its definition of
-- "trust corporation" is section-limited by its own terms and does not travel
-- to s 28. The two classes happen to have the same two members on the shared
-- ontology's `Person or corporation`, but that is a coincidence of this model
-- and not a proposition about the Act: s 28(2) says "a trust company"
-- unqualified where s 6(6) says "a corporation licensed as a trust company
-- under the Trust Companies Act 2005", so a trust company that is not so
-- licensed would be exempt from the oath and would not be a trust corporation
-- for s 6(2). An earlier draft called s 6(6) here and said only that the class
-- was s 6(6)'s under another name.
GIVEN pc IS A `Person or corporation`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 28(2) — the grantee is the Public Trustee or a trust company` pc MEANS
    CONSIDER pc
    WHEN `the Public Trustee` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `a corporation licensed as a trust company under the Trust Companies Act 2005` n THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 28(1), s 28(2)
GIVEN gr IS A Grantee
GIVETH A DEONTIC `A party under the Probate and Administration Act` `An act under the Probate and Administration Act`
`s 28 — the grantee shall take an oath` gr MEANS
    IF   `s 28(2) — the grantee is the Public Trustee or a trust company` (gr's `the grantee`)
    THEN FULFILLED
    ELSE PARTY `the grantee`
         MUST  `take an oath in the prescribed form, faithfully to administer the estate and to account for the same`
         HENCE FULFILLED

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 29(7) — "the person to whom the grant is made or on whose behalf it is sealed shall give security for the due administration of the estate"; s 55(1)(c)
-- THE ONE RULE IN THIS ACT WITH BOTH A DEADLINE AND A NAMED CONSEQUENCE, and
-- the two come from different sections: s 29(7) imposes the duty, s 55(1)(c)
-- dates it at one month from the grant and says what follows on default. The
-- LEST arm is therefore a PERMISSION and not a penalty — s 55(1) says letters
-- "may be granted to the Public Trustee" — so it is a MAY, and an unexercised
-- MAY collapses to FULFILLED rather than to a breach.
GIVEN `security is required` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A DEONTIC `A party under the Probate and Administration Act` `An act under the Probate and Administration Act`
`s 29(7) — the grantee shall give security for the due administration of the estate` `security is required` MEANS
    IF   `security is required`
    THEN PARTY  `the grantee`
         MUST   `give security for the due administration of the estate`
         WITHIN 30
         HENCE  FULFILLED
         LEST   PARTY  `the court`
                MAY    `grant letters of administration with or without the will annexed to the Public Trustee`
                WITHIN 30
    ELSE FULFILLED

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 12(2) — "the probate shall be revoked, and a new probate shall be granted of the will and codicil together"
-- MANDATORY, and stated as one act because s 12(2) makes revocation and
-- re-grant a single consequence of the same event: the probate does not simply
-- fall away, a new one issues of both instruments together.
GIVEN `the consequence` IS A `The consequence of a codicil propounded after a grant`
GIVETH A DEONTIC `A party under the Probate and Administration Act` `An act under the Probate and Administration Act`
`s 12(2) — the probate shall be revoked and a new probate granted` `the consequence` MEANS
    IF   `the consequence` EQUALS `the probate shall be revoked, and a new probate shall be granted of the will and codicil together`
    THEN PARTY `the court`
         MUST  `revoke the probate and grant a new probate of the will and codicil together`
         HENCE FULFILLED
    ELSE FULFILLED

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 27 — "letters of administration shall be granted to those persons to whom original grants might have been made"
-- MANDATORY. Contrast s 18(1) ("the court may grant letters of
-- administration") and s 25(2) ("letters of administration may be granted"),
-- which are on the same subject matter and are permissive; s 27 is the one
-- place in this Act where a further grant is compelled.
GIVEN d IS A `Deceased person`, g IS A Grant
GIVETH A DEONTIC `A party under the Probate and Administration Act` `An act under the Probate and Administration Act`
`s 27 — letters of administration shall be granted` d g MEANS
    IF   `s 27 — letters of administration shall be granted to those persons to whom original grants might have been made` d g
    THEN PARTY `the court`
         MUST  `grant letters of administration to those persons to whom original grants might have been made`
         HENCE FULFILLED
    ELSE FULFILLED


§ `What is NOT encoded, and why`

-- Everything named below is real statutory material in this Act. None of it is
-- encoded. A reader who cannot tell a provision that is not in the Act from
-- one that is merely not encoded has been misled, so this list is part of the
-- deliverable.
--
-- (1) DISCRETIONS — no determinate output, and a boolean for a discretion is a
--     lie. In each case the ANTECEDENT is encoded above where it is
--     determinate, and only the choice is left out.
--       s 5(2)   "the court may at any time allow the person so renouncing to
--                withdraw his renunciation ... if it is shown that the
--                withdrawal is for the benefit of the estate"
--       s 6(4)   "the court may ... appoint one or more personal
--                representatives in addition to the existing personal
--                representative" (the WINDOW is encoded; the appointment is not)
--       s 13(1)  "to such person or persons as the court considers the fittest
--                to administer the estate"
--       s 18(2)  "the court shall have regard to the rights of all persons
--                interested in the estate" (the settled-land limb IS encoded)
--       s 18(3)  "may be limited in any way the court thinks fit"
--       s 18(4)(b) "it shall be in the discretion of the court to grant them to
--                any one or more of such persons"
--       s 18(4)(d), s 20, s 22(2)(b), s 24, s 55(1) — each ends in "such person
--                as the court thinks fit" or its equivalent
--       s 24     "In any case in which it appears necessary for preserving the
--                property" (the LIMITATION is encoded; the gate is not)
--       s 26(1)  "subject to such exception as the will or the circumstances of
--                the case require" (s 26(2) IS encoded)
--       s 29(1)  "the registrar shall determine its sufficiency"
--       s 29(3)  "may for any sufficient reason increase or decrease the number
--                of the sureties, or dispense with them, or reduce the amount
--                of the bond"
--       s 29(6)  "Sureties may be required by the registrar to justify"
--       s 31     "The court may, if it appears that the condition of an
--                administration bond has been broken, order that it be
--                assigned"
--       s 32     "Any probate or letters of administration may be revoked or
--                amended for any sufficient cause" — the whole of Part 6, and
--                the clearest case in the Act: "any sufficient cause" has no
--                content a rule can test. The RESULT is in the ontology, as
--                `Grant`'s `revoked`, and this module reads it.
--       s 54     "if it appears that there is reason to believe that any will
--                ... is in the possession or under the control of any person"
--       s 62(1)  "may, if he thinks fit" (both gates ARE encoded)
--       s 62(5)  "may, if he thinks it expedient to do so ... decline to
--                proceed"
--       s 63(3)  "or otherwise as the Public Trustee may, in his discretion,
--                determine"
--       s 66(1)  "may in its or his discretion allow the executors or
--                administrators a commission not exceeding 5% on the value of
--                the assets collected by them" — and note that the 5% ceiling
--                IS determinate; what is not is whether any commission is
--                allowed at all, which the subsection makes turn on the court's
--                "approval or otherwise of their conduct".
--       s 67(1)  "reasonable testamentary and other expenses", "proper funeral
--                expenses", "all reasonable expenses of subsequent religious
--                ceremonies suitable to the station in life of the deceased"
--       s 69     the registrar's power in uncontested cases — a jurisdictional
--                allocation, not a test.
--
-- (2) MACHINERY THAT PRODUCES A FACT THE ONTOLOGY ALREADY HOLDS. Encoding the
--     procedure as well would give the same fact two homes.
--       ss 3-4   renunciation: the oral or attested writing (s 3(2)), the
--                citation, the notice of intention to contest and the deemed
--                renunciation for failing to file it (s 4(2)-(3)). The fact is
--                `has renounced the right to act as such`; s 5(1) IS encoded,
--                and is stated over a proved renunciation so that it reaches
--                s 3(1)'s whole class and not only appointed executors — see
--                the domain gap recorded at that site.
--       s 7      notation of domicile — the registrar's endorsement that the
--                deceased died domiciled in Singapore. The fact is
--                `domiciled in Singapore at the time of his death`.
--       s 33     caveats. The fact a caveat produces — that no grant shall be
--                made without notice to the caveator — is procedural and
--                nothing downstream in the grant machinery reads it.
--
-- (3) WHOLE PARTS OUT OF SCOPE, being the administration of the estate rather
--     than the grant of representation.
--       Part 4  (s 28) the oath. Its DUTY is stated above as a deontic; the
--               prescribed form is rules of court.
--       Part 6  (s 32) revocation of a grant — see (1).
--       Part 7  (s 33) caveats — see (2).
--       Part 8  (ss 34, 36) both repealed by Act 27 of 2014 and carrying no
--               operative words. Named above under Part 8 as well, so that an
--               in-order reading has no silent gap; s 35(1) IS encoded.
--       Part 9  (ss 37-45) protection of estates pending grant: vesting in the
--               Public Trustee, receivers, sale by order of court, the s 42
--               offence, the receiver's lien. Only s 39's gate is reproduced,
--               to the depth s 55(1)(d) needs.
--       Part 10 (ss 46-53) re-sealing of grants made outside Singapore. Note
--               that s 11, which IS encoded, is not part of this: s 11 makes a
--               FRESH Singapore grant on the footing of a foreign proving,
--               where s 47 gives a FOREIGN grant effect here.
--       ss 57-58, 66-68 and the First and Second Schedules: the administration
--               of assets, the insolvency route into the law of bankruptcy,
--               charges on property, commission, testamentary and funeral
--               expenses, and funds that cannot be immediately distributed.
--               These are the powers and accounts of personal representatives,
--               which the brief for this module puts outside it. The VALUES
--               they measure are on `Estate` in the shared ontology, so a later
--               module can state them without changing that file.
--
--               TWO THINGS FOLLOW FROM THIS OMISSION AND BOTH ARE OWED TO A
--               READER, because the omission is not local to this module.
--
--               (a) FIVE `Estate` FIELDS HAVE NO READER ANYWHERE. `family-domain.l4`
--               declares `the debts and liabilities of the deceased`, `the
--               value of the interest in property which at the time of his
--               death is charged with the payment of money`, `the amount of
--               the charge on that interest`, `the deceased has by will, deed
--               or other document signified a contrary or other intention` and
--               the s 58 facts beside them, and says why: "Only the INPUTS the
--               branch needs are here ... so that probate-administration-act.l4
--               can state s 57 completely." This module does not state s 57 at
--               all. Those fields are therefore written by fixtures and read by
--               nothing, in any of the four Act modules. Reported as a domain
--               gap: the ontology's justification names a consumer that does
--               not exist, and the right repair is in that file — either the
--               justification is corrected to say the fields are declared for a
--               s 57/s 58 module not yet written, or such a module is written.
--               Not repaired here; three other Act modules are relying on that
--               file being stable.
--
--               (b) family-domain ALSO INSTRUCTS THIS MODULE TO DO SOMETHING IT
--               DOES NOT DO. At `Creditor` it says of `the debts and
--               liabilities of the deceased`: "Where both are present the PAA
--               module should check them against each other rather than
--               silently preferring one." No such check exists here, and there
--               is nowhere honest to put one: the check belongs where the
--               aggregate is READ, which is s 57, which is out of scope. The
--               fixtures show the unchecked pairing plainly — several set the
--               aggregate to 0 beside a creditor owed thousands — and that is a
--               fixture convenience under the standing rule that a 0 here means
--               "not in evidence in this scenario". Reported rather than
--               discharged.
--
--               (c) THE INTESTATE SUCCESSION ACT RELIES ON ss 57-58 BY NAME.
--               Its s 5 distributes the property "after payment thereout of the
--               expenses of due administration as prescribed by the Probate and
--               Administration Act 1934" — a pointer at this Act, and at these
--               two sections and the First Schedule in particular. That module
--               discharges its side by deducting `the funeral, testamentary and
--               administration expenses` and saying why it deducts that figure
--               and not the debts. This module cannot answer, because the
--               provisions the pointer names are out of scope. Said here so
--               that the pointer does not run into silence: it is not that this
--               Act declines to prescribe, it is that this MODULE stops short
--               of the sections that do.
--       ss 59-61 and the Third Schedule: administration by consular officers of
--               the seven States named there. Out of scope, and note that the
--               Third Schedule is itself variable by the Minister (s 60), so a
--               hard-coded list would go stale.
--       s 65    interest payable into the Consolidated Fund.
--       s 70    the 8 March 2004 transitional provision substituting the Public
--               Trustee for the official assignee.
--       s 1     short title.
--
-- (4) CONSEQUENCES AND PROCEDURE HANGING OFF A SUBSECTION THAT IS ENCODED.
--     Each is a step that follows a decision rather than a test that makes one,
--     so there is nothing for a rule to answer.
--       s 56(1)  the payment out of funds IN COURT on the death of a payee —
--                orders, certificates of the registrar, the trustee/own-right
--                distinction. It is not grant machinery; s 56(2), which is the
--                $500 route that presupposes no administration, IS encoded.
--       s 62(4)  "Notice of every declaration made under subsection (1) shall
--                be given to the registrar." — the notice that follows the s 62(1) declaration.
--                s 62(1)-(3) ARE encoded; s 62(5) is at (1) above.
--       s 64(2)-(3). s 64(1) IS encoded. s 64(2) provides that the Public
--                Trustee "shall not, as respects the property conveyed or
--                distributed under subsection (1), be liable to any person of
--                whose claim the Public Trustee has not had notice at the time
--                of conveyance or distribution", and s 64(3) saves the right
--                "to follow the property representing the same" and the duty
--                "to make searches similar to those which an intending
--                purchaser would be advised to make or obtain". Both are
--                consequences of a distribution having been made, not gates on
--                making one.
--
-- (5) ONE PROVISION LEFT OUT FOR A REASON OF ITS OWN.
--       s 35(2)-(3). s 35(1) IS encoded. s 35(2) makes the grantee liable for
--         estate duty under an Act not in this cleanroom and for "such court
--         fees as would have been payable had the application ... been made to
--         the General Division of the High Court", and requires security
--         measured by that court's practice — two quantities this module cannot
--         compute. s 35(3) then makes revocation available "Unless in such case
--         the grantee complies without delay with the requirements of
--         subsection (2)", and "without delay" fixes no period.


§ `Where this Act reaches into the other three`

-- SIX JOINS. The first of them is ONE fact seen from four provisions of this
-- Act; the other five are five different facts.
--
-- WHAT A JOIN OWES. Each entry below says what the other Act does, what this
-- module does about it, and — where this module does nothing — why not. A
-- silent side is worse than a wrong one: a reader cannot tell silence from a
-- considered decision that the join does not matter. Two of these six were
-- silent on this side until a cross-module review found them, and they are
-- marked.
--
-- (1) GUARDIANSHIP OF INFANTS ACT 1934 — INFANCY. A "minority" (s 6(2)),
--     an "infant" (s 21), "the person for whose use and benefit the grant is
--     made is an infant" (s 29(8)(a)) and "during the minority of any such
--     person" (s 63(1)) are all one question, and none of the four Acts
--     answers it: no Act in this cleanroom defines an infant, and the age of
--     majority comes from outside them. The shared ontology therefore records
--     `the date on which the person attains the age of majority` as an
--     already-construed input, and this module compares it with a date. The
--     Guardianship Act module reads the SAME field and compares it the same
--     strict way, so where the date is in evidence the two agree exactly,
--     boundary included. Where it is not, the two part: that module returns
--     `MAYBE BOOLEAN` and yields NOTHING, and this one folds to FALSE. See
--     AMBIGUITY A2, which now also records what the Wills Act does with the
--     same evidential question and why all three answers differ.
--
--     THE WILLS ACT IS NOT PART OF THIS JOIN, and that is correct. Its s 4
--     states an age in its own words — "No will made by any person under 21
--     years of age shall be valid" — and its s 27(1) disapplies s 4 for a
--     soldier or mariner, so its answer must come from the Act and not from an
--     outside construction. It computes its own and does not read the shared
--     field. Three modules hold that the age is an input; the fourth is right
--     not to.
--
-- (2) GUARDIANSHIP OF INFANTS ACT 1934 — THE OFFICE OF GUARDIAN. s 21(2)
--     grants to "the guardian of the person and property of the infant". That
--     office is created by the Guardianship Act (its ss 6, 7 and 10) and by a
--     will (its s 7(1)-(2), and Wills Act s 27(3)); this Act creates none and
--     defines none. So this module can decide WHETHER a grant may be made to
--     the infant's guardian and cannot decide WHO that guardian is.
--
--     THIS SIDE WAS SILENT UNTIL A CROSS-MODULE REVIEW. The sentence above was
--     here and the s 21(2) rule read none of the three fields the ontology
--     provides for the WHETHER — `Grantee`'s capacity and its pointer to the
--     person for whose use and benefit the grant is made, and `Infant`'s
--     `the guardians of the infant` — so the decision named for the guardian
--     answered the same for a grant to a stranger. It now reads all three, and
--     tests the KIND of guardianship (s 21(2) says "of the person and
--     property") and whether the court has removed him under Guardianship Act
--     s 10. It still does not read HOW he was appointed: that is the other
--     Act's question and the answer arrives construed.
--
-- (3) INTESTATE SUCCESSION ACT 1967 — WHO THE NEXT OF KIN ARE. s 18(4)(a),
--     s 18(4)(d)(i) and s 18(4)(d)(ii) all speak of "next of kin" and this Act
--     never says who they are. s 13(2)(c) reaches the same class by a
--     counterfactual, and s 19 by the phrase "a person entitled to letters of
--     administration in case of intestacy". The class is the Intestate
--     Succession Act's; see AMBIGUITY A3 for WHICH classes and whether they
--     are ranked, and AMBIGUITY A12 for whether that Act's DEFINITIONS come
--     with them. They do: a person who is not a "child" within its s 3 is not
--     next of kin here, and neither are his descendants.
--
--     THE TWO MODULES NOW AGREE ON THE SAME PERSON. Before A12 was decided
--     this module walked the raw `children` edge, so an intestate's child who
--     was neither legitimate nor adopted was next of kin here and took nothing
--     there — the same person, the same estate, two answers. `wills-act.l4`
--     reads its own s 26 the other way and is right to; the reason the two
--     modules diverge is set out at A12 and at that module's site.
--
--     s 6(4)'s "minority of a beneficiary" reaches the same way on an
--     INTESTACY, where who the beneficiaries are is that Act's question and
--     not this one's; this module answers it with s 18(4)(a)'s class as a
--     stated proxy, and records at that site which way the proxy errs.
--
-- (4) WILLS ACT 1838 — THE WILL ITSELF. Every provision here that begins
--     with a Where-clause about a will presupposes a valid will, and validity
--     is Wills Act
--     s 6's question, not this Act's. This module reads `Will` and never
--     validates it. In the other direction, Wills Act s 28(2) measures its
--     rectification deadline off the first grant made under THIS Act, and
--     s 28(4)-(5) filter and re-date that grant — which is why
--     `Limitation on the grant` in the shared ontology is stated in the Wills
--     Act's categories and this module produces values of it (ss 14, 17, 19,
--     20, 21, 22, 23, 24).
--
-- (5) INTESTATE SUCCESSION ACT 1967 — THE EXPENSES OF DUE ADMINISTRATION.
--     THIS SIDE WAS SILENT UNTIL A CROSS-MODULE REVIEW, and the pointer runs
--     INTO this Act rather than out of it. That Act's s 5 distributes the
--     property "after payment thereout of the expenses of due administration
--     as prescribed by the Probate and Administration Act 1934". The
--     provisions that prescribe them are, on the face of this Act, the First
--     Schedule para 1 ("The funeral, testamentary and administration expenses
--     shall have priority.", given effect by s 57(1) for an insolvent estate),
--     s 57(4) with the Second Schedule for a solvent one, and s 67(1) ("The
--     court shall allow the executors or administrators the reasonable
--     testamentary and other expenses incurred by them, and also proper
--     funeral expenses ..."). This module puts every one of them out of scope.
--     So the answer to that Act's question is not here, and the reason is the
--     scope of this MODULE and not a gap in this Act. Set out in full, with
--     the consequences for the `Estate` fields the shared ontology declares
--     for those sections, in the closing "What is NOT encoded, and why",
--     group (3).
--
-- (6) INTESTATE SUCCESSION ACT 1967 — s 29(5)'s ADMINISTRATOR WHO IS
--     "entitled to the whole of the estate after payment of the debts". The
--     same fact under two names, and nothing joins them. That Act
--     computes who takes what and produces `Entitlement`; this module takes
--     s 29(5)'s antecedent as a sworn boolean because no field anywhere has
--     that type. A case file may carry both and have them contradict, and on
--     the contradiction the sureties come off an administration bond. Stated
--     as a domain gap at the field itself, with what would close it.


§ `Ambiguity register`

-- Every fork below is marked at the site where it bites, with both readings,
-- the reading taken and why. This table and those comments must agree.
--
--   A1  s 13(1)(c), s 25(1), s 55(1)(f) — what "survives" requires when a date
--       of death is missing. Proof, or presumption. TAKEN: proof.
--   A2  s 6(2), s 21, s 29(8)(a), s 63(1) — a person whose date of majority is
--       not in evidence. Not an infant, or presumed an infant. TAKEN: not
--       established, s 6(3) being the Act's own route for the evidential
--       question. The one fork in this register that a NEIGHBOURING Act
--       answers the other way: `wills-act.l4` gives its s 4 a distinct
--       not-in-evidence verdict and requires validity to be affirmatively
--       established, and `guardianship-of-infants-act.l4` refuses to fold at
--       all. Both differences are stated at the A2 site with the reason.
--   A3  s 13(2)(c), s 18(4)(a), s 18(4)(d), s 19 — who "the next of kin" are.
--       The Intestate Succession Act's classes flat, or its nearest class
--       only. TAKEN: flat.
--   A4  s 6(2) — whose minority. Anyone concerned in the estate, or the
--       beneficiaries only. TAKEN: anyone concerned.
--   A5  s 6(2) — which grants it governs. Both kinds of letters of
--       administration, or letters simpliciter only. TAKEN: both.
--   A6  s 13(1)(b) — how "or have renounced" distributes. Per executor, or per
--       class. TAKEN: per executor.
--   A7  s 18(1) — what "died intestate" means here. No will at all, or the
--       Intestate Succession Act's inclusive definition. TAKEN: no will at all.
--   A8  s 18(4)(d)(i) — "the greater number of such next of kin". A majority of
--       all, or a majority of those with a view. TAKEN: a majority of all.
--   A9  s 25(1) — "except in cases to which section 6 applies". The s 6(2)
--       cases, or every case. TAKEN: the s 6(2) cases.
--   A10 s 13(1) — the five limbs where the will appoints no executor. All five
--       are vacuously satisfied, or limbs (b) to (e) presuppose an executor.
--       TAKEN: vacuous, because limb (a) is satisfied in exactly that case and
--       the disjunction gives the same answer either way.
--   A11 s 30 — which grants "letters of administration" reaches. Both kinds, or
--       letters simpliciter only. TAKEN: both. This is A5's fork asked again of
--       a different section, and it is answered the same way for a DIFFERENT
--       reason: A5's turns on s 6(2)'s life-interest limb, which s 30 has not,
--       and A11's turns on s 13(2)(e) expressly ranking a creditor for a grant
--       with the will annexed.
--   A12 s 13(2)(c), s 18(4)(a), s 18(4)(d), s 19 — whether the Intestate
--       Succession Act 1967's DEFINITION of "child" (its s 3) travels into
--       "next of kin" along with the classes A3 borrows, or whether "In this
--       Act" confines it and kinship in fact governs. TAKEN: it travels, at
--       every generation, a failing branch dropping entire. A3's second half.
--       It is the one place where this module and `wills-act.l4` answer the
--       same words differently and the divergence is deliberate: that module
--       reads its own s 26 as descent because s 26 borrows nothing, and this
--       one borrows, so it takes what it borrowed. Stated at both sites.
--
-- TWO THINGS THAT ARE NOT AMBIGUITIES BUT ARE NOTED WITH THEM, because a
-- reader looking for the fork will look here.
--
--   s 6(4)'s "the minority of a beneficiary" is NOT s 6(2)'s "a minority", and
--   A4 rules on s 6(2) only. s 6(4) has its own narrower predicate,
--   `s 6(4) — there is a minority of a beneficiary`, for the reason given at
--   that site. Its "beneficiary" is also narrower than "a person who takes
--   something under the will": a charge or direction for the payment of a debt
--   is not a benefit, on the same `beneficial` filter s 13(2)(c) and s 13(2)(d)
--   apply.
--
--   s 29(8) asks A5's and A11's question a third time — which grants "letters
--   of administration" reaches — and it is NOT a fork, because s 29(7) answers
--   it: the duty s 29(8) excuses is imposed "in the case of administrations
--   whether with or without the will annexed" and is expressly "Subject to
--   subsection (8)". Both kinds, on the Act's words. Stated at the s 29(8)
--   site, where the direction of the reading is also recorded.


§ `Worked examples`

-- THE FIXTURES ARE BUILT BY PARAMETERISED CONSTRUCTORS, because L4 has no
-- record-update operator: `value WITH field IS x` works only on a TYPE name,
-- never on an existing record. So each builder below spells every field once
-- and the scenarios vary only the operative ones.
--
-- EVERY CONSTRUCTOR IN `Constructors` CARRIES THE TAG "PAA", AND NOTHING ELSE
-- IN THIS FILE DOES. The four Act modules in this directory could not be
-- imported together: they build the same shared records — a `Deceased person`,
-- a `Family`, an `Estate`, a `Grant`, a `Child` — and three of them had
-- independently reached for `a deceased person`, `a family`, `an estate`,
-- `a grant` and `a legitimate child` as the names, and L4 raises a hard error
-- at the first USE of a name two imported modules define. Nothing could
-- therefore be written that saw all four Acts at once, which is the whole
-- point of encoding four Acts over one family.
--
-- The convention is: the tag goes immediately after the article, and it goes
-- on every parameterised builder in this subsection whether or not that
-- particular name collided today, so that a reader of a module importing all
-- four can tell at a glance whose fixture layer he is looking at. Measured
-- before and after: this module shared five top-level names with its
-- neighbours and now shares none, pairwise, across all four Act modules.
-- Nothing else was renamed. The statutory decisions keep their own words —
-- a reader holding the Act finds the rule by reading the section heading, and
-- that is the deliverable.
--
-- THE TAG IS A WORKAROUND AND IS REPORTED AS A DOMAIN GAP. `a deceased
-- person`, `a family`, `an estate`, `a grant` and `a legitimate child` build
-- types that `family-domain.l4` owns, and they belong with the types, built
-- once. Prefixing per module means four builders for one record and four
-- places for a default to drift. The right home is a shared fixture module
-- that owns them; that file does not exist, and this module does not create
-- it, because three other Act modules are relying on the shared file being
-- stable.
--
-- THE DEFAULTS ARE FIXTURE CONVENIENCES AND CARRY NO LEGAL CLAIM. A field set
-- to FALSE or 0 here means "not in evidence in this scenario", not "the Act
-- says so".
--
-- ONE DATE GOVERNS EVERY TIME-SENSITIVE EVALUATION, so the file evaluates the
-- same way tomorrow as today. Nothing below reads a clock.

`the date of the hearing` MEANS YMD 2026 1 1

§§ `Constructors`

GIVEN `the name` IS A STRING
      `the reference` IS A STRING
      `the vital status` IS A `Vital status`
      `the date of majority` IS A MAYBE DATE
      `the children of the person` IS A LIST OF Child
      `does lack mental capacity` IS A BOOLEAN
      `is absent from Singapore` IS A BOOLEAN
      `is willing to act` IS A BOOLEAN
      `does so desire` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A Person
`a PAA person in full` `the name` `the reference` `the vital status` `the date of majority` `the children of the person` `does lack mental capacity` `is absent from Singapore` `is willing to act` `does so desire` MEANS
    Person WITH
        `name` IS `the name`
        `the reference for this person` IS `the reference`
        `date of birth` IS NOTHING
        `vital status` IS `the vital status`
        `children` IS `the children of the person`
        `at the date of the death only conceived in the womb but subsequently born alive` IS FALSE
        `money or other property given, paid or settled by the intestate during his life to or for the advancement of the person` IS 0
        `the date on which the person attains the age of majority` IS `the date of majority`
        `a soldier being in actual military service` IS FALSE
        `a mariner or seaman being at sea` IS FALSE
        `a member of any naval or marine forces so circumstanced that if he were a soldier he would be in actual military service` IS FALSE
        `so desires that letters of administration be granted to such person as the court thinks fit` IS `does so desire`
        `lacks mental capacity (within the meaning of the Mental Capacity Act 2008) to make decisions with respect to the management and administration of the person's property and affairs` IS `does lack mental capacity`
        `absent from Singapore` IS `is absent from Singapore`
        `willing to act` IS `is willing to act`

-- A person of full age, living, with no children of record.
GIVEN `the name` IS A STRING, `the reference` IS A STRING
GIVETH A Person
`a PAA adult` `the name` `the reference` MEANS
    `a PAA person in full` `the name` `the reference` `living` (JUST (YMD 2005 1 1)) EMPTY FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE

-- An infant: majority falls after `the date of the hearing`.
GIVEN `the name` IS A STRING, `the reference` IS A STRING
GIVETH A Person
`a PAA minor` `the name` `the reference` MEANS
    `a PAA person in full` `the name` `the reference` `living` (JUST (YMD 2032 6 1)) EMPTY FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE

-- A person whose age is not in evidence at all — AMBIGUITY A2's case.
GIVEN `the name` IS A STRING, `the reference` IS A STRING
GIVETH A Person
`a PAA person of unknown age` `the name` `the reference` MEANS
    `a PAA person in full` `the name` `the reference` `living` NOTHING EMPTY FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE

GIVEN p IS A Person
GIVETH A Child
`a PAA legitimate child` p MEANS
    Child WITH
        `the child` IS p
        `a legitimate child of the person whose child he is` IS TRUE
        `adopted by that person by virtue of an order of court under any written law for the time being in force in Singapore, Malaysia or Brunei Darussalam` IS FALSE

-- THE OTHER TWO CORNERS OF Intestate Succession Act s 3, which AMBIGUITY A12
-- makes operative here. An adopted child satisfies the definition on its
-- second limb; a child who is neither legitimate nor adopted satisfies
-- neither, and on A12's reading is not next of kin however plainly he is the
-- deceased's child in fact. Without these two constructors nothing in this
-- file could produce the case A12 decides, and the ruling would be asserted
-- nowhere.
GIVEN p IS A Person
GIVETH A Child
`a PAA adopted child` p MEANS
    Child WITH
        `the child` IS p
        `a legitimate child of the person whose child he is` IS FALSE
        `adopted by that person by virtue of an order of court under any written law for the time being in force in Singapore, Malaysia or Brunei Darussalam` IS TRUE

GIVEN p IS A Person
GIVETH A Child
`a PAA child neither legitimate nor adopted` p MEANS
    Child WITH
        `the child` IS p
        `a legitimate child of the person whose child he is` IS FALSE
        `adopted by that person by virtue of an order of court under any written law for the time being in force in Singapore, Malaysia or Brunei Darussalam` IS FALSE

